/* Generated by scripts/build-manual-index.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
window.MANUAL_INDEX = [{"url":"appendices.html#glossary","title":"Glossary","section":"appendices","text":"Node One card on the canvas. Each does one thing: send a message, build a machine, pay the player. Wire The line joining two nodes. Wires decide what runs and in what order; where the nodes sit decides nothing. Socket The point a wire attaches to. Every socket takes one kind of wire and refuses the others. Palette The list of nodes down the left. Drag one out, or click it to drop one in the middle. Inspector The panel on the right, with its Node , Quest and Mod tabs. Field One setting or control on a node. It might be a text box, number, switch, list, colour or another kind of control. Hint A short explanation attached to a field. It may appear under the control or when you hover. This handb"},{"url":"appendices.html#shortcuts","title":"Keyboard and mouse","section":"appendices","text":"Most keyboard shortcuts work across the editor. Copy, cut, paste, duplicate and delete do not act while you are typing. Shift + A and Ctrl + G are canvas actions. Mouse gestures are also listed. Open Shortcuts in the editor to see its list. Editing Undo Ctrl Z Redo Ctrl Shift Z or Ctrl Y Copy / cut / paste the selected nodes Ctrl C · X · V Duplicate the selected nodes Ctrl D Delete the selected nodes or wires Delete Save the draft now Ctrl S Adding nodes Place a node where you drop it drag from the palette Add a node at the viewport centre click a palette card Search for a node at the pointer Shift A Search for a node here right-click the canvas Create a node the wire fits drag a wire onto e"},{"url":"appendices.html#limits","title":"What this editor cannot do yet","section":"appendices","text":"Stated plainly, because each of these has cost somebody an afternoon. None is a bug; each is a boundary, and the editor does not always tell you when you cross one. It cannot give an objective two triggers Only the first event wired to a trigger is used. A second one is ignored without a message. If you need two ways to finish, use two objectives. It will not finish a quest without a finish path Add Complete quest to a success ending, or switch on Complete automatically . On quest complete runs after that path. It cannot fix Bettercap's access-point name display Create Wi-Fi now ships as a real access point. Bettercap may show no network name if you target one by BSSID in the current game bu"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-list","title":"The 99 events","section":"appendices","text":"This list groups the 99 built-in events a When event node can wait for. A loaded addon can add more. To find a built-in event, type part of its name into the node's Game event picker."},{"url":"appendices.html#events-recon","title":"Reconnaissance & terminal (18)","section":"appendices","text":"Terminal.Cat Terminal.Cd Terminal.Command Terminal.Dig Terminal.DnsHistory Terminal.Explorer Terminal.Geoip Terminal.Ifconfig Terminal.InstallPackage Terminal.Ls Terminal.Lynx.Lookup Terminal.Lynx.Search Terminal.Mxlookup Terminal.NmapScan Terminal.Nslookup Terminal.Openssl Terminal.Ping Terminal.Whois"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-web","title":"Directory brute-force, browser & HTTP (7)","section":"appendices","text":"Browser.Meta Browser.WebsiteOpened Http.CollaboratorHit Http.Intercepted Http.Request Http.Response Terminal.Dirhunter"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-access","title":"Access & exploitation (19)","section":"appendices","text":"Metasploit.Event Metasploit.Event.Try Metasploit.Meterpreter.Connected Metasploit.Msfconsole Metasploit.Rootgrab Metasploit.Search Metasploit.SetOption Metasploit.ShowOptions Metasploit.Use Meterpreter.Download RemoteConnection.Disconnected RemoteConnection.Established Terminal.FTP.Connect Terminal.Hydra Terminal.Hydra.Try Terminal.SSH.Connected Terminal.SSH.Disconnected Terminal.SSH.FileDownload Terminal.SSH.Shutdown"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-cracking","title":"Cracking & vuln scanning (9)","section":"appendices","text":"Fern.FindPassword Hashcat John.DecryptHash Nuclei.Item Nuclei.Results Sqlmap.DumpTable Sqlmap.ListTables Subfinder.Results Subfinder.Try"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-wifi","title":"Bettercap & Wi-Fi (8)","section":"appendices","text":"Bettercap.Close Bettercap.NetProbe Bettercap.NetShow Bettercap.Open Bettercap.WifiDeAuth Bettercap.WifiRecon Network.WifiConnected Network.WifiDisconnected"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-network","title":"Network & infrastructure (7)","section":"appendices","text":"Database.Connected Database.DataUpdate Network.PortChanges Network.UserActivity NetworkPacketTransfer PFSense.Changes PFSense.Login"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-files","title":"Files (4)","section":"appendices","text":"Files.Deleted Files.Open Files.Transfer Python3.ExecFile"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-mail","title":"E-mail (6)","section":"appendices","text":"Mail.AccountCreated Mail.AccountLoggedIn Mail.MailboxOpened Mail.Read Mail.Received Mail.Sent"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-social","title":"Social & chat (11)","section":"appendices","text":"Kisscord.FriendAdded Kisscord.Messaging Twotter.AccountCreated Twotter.AccountLogin Twotter.AccountLogout Twotter.Post Twotter.PostSeen Twotter.ProfileSeen WeeChat.Connected WeeChat.Disconnected WeeChat.Message"},{"url":"appendices.html#events-world","title":"Bank, quest & misc (10)","section":"appendices","text":"AppStore.Downloaded Bank.AccountCreated Bank.Logout Bank.Transfer BCC.News.Opened Game.SessionStarted Process.Killed Quest.Claimed Wireshark.Started Wireshark.Stopped Names are dotted and capitalised. They come from the game, not from this editor, so they read like Terminal.Lynx.Search rather than like a sentence. You never type one by hand."},{"url":"appendices.html#version","title":"Which handbook version is this","section":"appendices","text":"This handbook documents editor build 2026-10-03.r267 . The same stamp appears in the footer of every page, and the editor writes it into every mod it exports. To check your editor Open the canvas Debug panel. The build stamp is the first thing in it. To check a mod you exported Open dist/mod.js inside the .zip . The stamp is in the header comment. See the build stamp . Why it matters A handbook for an older build can describe a field that has moved. If your stamp is newer than this one, the pages may still be right. Check the export window for what ships. ← When something goes wrong HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-10-03.r267 · every figure on these pages is taken "},{"url":"checking.html#where-messages-appear","title":"Where messages appear","section":"checking","text":"On the node A small badge on the card, visible from across the canvas. This is how you find a problem in a quest too big to read node by node. In the inspector Under the field the message is about, when you have that node selected. Field messages only show while the node is selected. They cannot nag at you from the other side of the canvas. On the canvas, as a chip A small counter beside the canvas tools. It reads No issues or shows how many problems are blocking and how many are to review . Move your mouse over the counter to read the full messages and next steps. Red marks an item the editor counts as blocking; amber marks something to review. “Blocking” means the editor sees a serious ris"},{"url":"checking.html#levels","title":"How to read the colours and labels","section":"checking","text":"On the canvas and in the inspector, amber means review this ; red means the editor sees a serious risk. In the export report, blue marks a note, amber a warning and red an error. If any report item is an error, the heading says Needs attention ; otherwise it says Good to know . Those headings do not lock the download button. Read the full sentence; do not rely on colour alone. These checks have limits: for example, the red No trigger warning checks for a game event, not a story wire. Some amber export notes also matter; one can say that files will be dropped. A clean report cannot prove that the quest plays well. See Dry run for a first check, then test in game."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-index","title":"Messages on the canvas and in the inspector","section":"checking","text":"Names and values in these examples can vary from quest to quest. Search for the stable words from the message you see; use the explanation below to decide what to do."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-unreachable","title":"Unreachable","section":"checking","text":"Nothing leads to this node. Wire it to the chain that should run it — nodes do nothing until something points at them. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means The node is on the canvas and is never run. A node only does something when another node points at it. The exception is a lifecycle node, which starts a chain by itself. What to do Drag a wire from the node that should run it into this node's input. That is usually the last node of your Quest start chain. If the node is left over from an idea you dropped, delete it instead."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-no-trigger","title":"No trigger","section":"checking","text":"Nothing completes this objective. Wire a “When event” node into its trigger socket, or the player can never finish the quest. Colour Red Where On the objective, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means This check looks for a When event wire. It misses story wires. The game can also tick a plain objective when the story reaches it. So this warning may appear even when that route works. What to do Should an in-game event complete this objective? Add a When event node. Connect its When socket to the objective's Trigger socket. If a story wire should tick it, check that route in the Dry run . The warning may be a false alarm."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-dead-end","title":"Dead end","section":"checking","text":"One output or step has no wire. What happens next depends on the node. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means One route or step has no next wire. The message names the missing outcome or step. It may say Yes , No , Installed , Missing , Correct , Wrong or a step name you chose. On App Install Check The player stalls if the desktop check takes the unwired route. Connect that route, or send both routes to the same next step if the result does not matter. On Ask player An unwired Submitted , Correct , Wrong or Cancelled route has no follow-up. That is fine if the story should stop there; otherwise wire that route onward. On a Sequence The named step has no w"},{"url":"checking.html#msg-nothing-scheduled","title":"Nothing scheduled","section":"checking","text":"No time (or no full date) is set, so the timer fires the moment the story reaches it — nothing waits. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means The Timer has no time set, so it does not actually wait: the story carries straight on through it. That is probably not the timer you meant to build. What to do Set a wait in Years , Months , Weeks , Days , Hours or Minutes . Or set a full date using Year , Month and Day . Remove the node if you do not need it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-timer-nothing-to-do","title":"The timer has nothing to do","section":"checking","text":"When the time comes, the timer has nowhere to go — the story stops there. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means The Timer has a time, but nothing is wired to its Out socket. When the time comes, the timer fires and the story has no next step. What to do Wire the Out socket to the node that should run when the time comes. Leave it unwired if the story is meant to end there."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-only-runs-on-completion","title":"Only runs on completion","section":"checking","text":"This is wired, but it only runs after the quest is marked complete. This graph has no Complete quest node yet. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means You have built follow-up work on On quest complete , but the graph has no visible finish node. That path waits until something marks the quest complete. What to do Add Complete quest to the final story beat, or use Complete automatically or the player's Complete button from Behaviour."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-empty","title":"Empty","section":"checking","text":"Nothing is wired to “…” That is fine if you do not need it — delete the node to clear this. Colour Amber Where On the node, and in the canvas issue chip. What it means One of the four lifecycle nodes is on the canvas with nothing after it, so it runs and changes nothing. The message names the node in the quotes. What to do Wire the nodes that should run at that moment to its output. On Quest start that is usually your briefing message first. If you do not need the node, delete it and the message goes with it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-app-no-name","title":"No app name","section":"checking","text":"No app name, so this check can never match anything. Colour Amber Where In the inspector, under App name . What it means An App Install Check set to check one app has no name to look for. The game cannot report an app as installed without a name, so the Missing path runs every time. Whether that is a problem depends on the route you intended. What to do Type the app's name exactly as the desktop spells it. The list-saving option is separate; it does not replace this check's app name."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-no-machine","title":"No machine set","section":"checking","text":"No machine set — this node does nothing until it points at one. Colour Amber Where In the inspector, under Device IP or Protected IP . What it means A Change port or Firewall rule node has no machine to act on. The target field is blank. The editor cannot tell whether you left it empty on purpose. This node has no machine to act on until you choose one. What to do Type the target address token to aim it at the machine your network created. On a firewall rule, pick Random — the quest's network instead."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-no-network","title":"No network creates that machine","section":"checking","text":"This is set to a machine, but no network in this quest creates one for it to act on. Colour Amber Where In the inspector, under the address field. What it means The address is filled in, and nothing in the quest brings a machine with that address into the world. The node points at a machine that will not exist. What to do Add a Create network node from World building and point this at a device in it. If you do not need the node, remove it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-network","title":"Files aimed at a network that does not exist","section":"checking","text":"This “Place files” node puts files on a remote device, but no network in this quest exists for that device to live on — export would drop the files. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The files have nowhere to land. The project can still export, but the exported mod will not include those files at that address. The player may be missing a clue. What to do Add a Create network node and give it the device this node is aimed at."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-device","title":"Files aimed at an address nothing uses","section":"checking","text":"No device in this quest uses that address, so these files would be dropped when you export. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The address is typed in and matches no device your network creates. With no address at all, the editor writes No address set, so the files have nowhere to land instead. Either way, export drops the files. What to do Follow the message's next step. It may list addresses your quest creates, or tell you to use the same address token you gave the network."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-files-no-user","title":"Files aimed at a device with no account","section":"checking","text":"That device has no user account, and files mount under a user's home folder — export would drop the files. Colour Red Where In the inspector, under Device IP . What it means The device exists, but it has no user account with a home folder for the files. Export drops them. What to do Add a user account to that device in your Create network node."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-prompt-answer","title":"A question has no accepted answer","section":"checking","text":"This question is set to check the answer, but no accepted answer is filled in yet. An “Ask player” node checks the answer, but “Answer to accept” is blank. Where Under Answer to accept on the selected Ask player node. The export report also repeats this warning. What it means The node is set to compare what the player types, but there is no answer to compare it with. Every answer misses. What to do Fill in Answer to accept , or change Accept to Any submitted text if any answer should continue the story."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-app-command","title":"A terminal command is not a desktop app","section":"checking","text":"The game does not count lynx as an app, so this check always reads Missing even when it is installed. Where Under App name on the selected App Install Check node. What it means The node checks apps on the player's desktop, not commands. The game does not count lynx as a desktop app, even when installed. What to do Check a desktop app instead, such as Kisscord. The game always takes the Missing path for a terminal command, even when it is installed."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-field-timer-invalid-date","title":"A calendar date that never comes","section":"checking","text":"“31 June” never arrives — June has 30 days. Where On the day field of a Timer set to wait for a fixed date, while that node is selected. What it means The full date is filled in, but that month does not have the chosen day. The timer cannot reach it. What to do Choose a day that exists in that month, or change the month to one that has the day you want."},{"url":"checking.html#panel-messages","title":"Messages inside a panel","section":"checking","text":"These ten messages come from panels, not canvas checks. They appear beside the item they describe. Check the nearby item in your project. Message values may name specific items."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-objective-trigger","title":"Health flags an Objective with no event trigger","section":"checking","text":"1 objective has no trigger wired in, so the player can never complete it. Where The Quest tab, in Health at the bottom. What it means The panel counts objectives without a When event wire. It does not check story wires. A plain objective can still tick when the story reaches it. This count may include an objective that works by story flow. What to do Should an in-game event complete this objective? Connect a When event to its Trigger socket. If a story wire should tick it, check the route in the Dry run . This panel may be counting a false alarm. See Objectives for both routes."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-router-no-way-in","title":"A router with no recognized recovery route","section":"checking","text":"This router has no way in: set a model for `fern`, or enable support-mail recovery. Where In the device tree, under the router. What it means The editor checks for a router model that supports fern recovery or support-mail recovery. It cannot check every route. Review the ones you planned. What to do If the player should use fern , give the router a model. If they should get credentials by e-mail, switch on support-mail recovery. Test any other route in game."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-ssh-guest","title":"An exploit that lands in the wrong account","section":"checking","text":"The SSH exploit lands in a guest account when it finds one, so the player may need to switch user or crack a password to reach the accounts you wrote. Turn this off for a machine that should have only the accounts listed here. Where In the device tree, under a machine with the exploit enabled. What it means This is a reminder, not a fault. The player may enter as the guest account instead of the account that holds your files. They may need to switch accounts or find another way in. What to do Leave it on if switching user is part of the puzzle. Turn it off if the machine should hold only the accounts you listed."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-table-no-name","title":"A database table the game will skip","section":"checking","text":"This table has no name, so the game will skip it. Where Under a table in a database editor. What it means The rows you typed will not exist in the game. The player will search a table that was never created. What to do Name the table. The rows survive as soon as it has one."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-event-detail","title":"A detail missing from the editor's event list","section":"checking","text":"Not one of this event's known details Where A small amber warning icon beside a condition's value field. Hover it for this text. What it means The detail you typed is not in the editor's list for this event. The condition may never match if the game does not send that detail. What to do Pick a detail from the list the editor offers. If the detail you need is absent, check the game's event information or choose another event. This warning compares against the editor's known details; it cannot inspect a live game event."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-addon-not-set-up","title":"An addon card that runs nothing","section":"checking","text":"Not set up yet — this card doesn't know which tool action it runs. Delete it and drag the action in again from the palette's “Editor Mods” group. As it stands it does nothing. Where On an addon card in the inspector. What it means The card has no tool action selected. This can happen with an incomplete or older card; it does not prove that the addon failed to load. What to do If the addon is not loaded, load it first. Then remove this card and drag the action in again from the palette. The existing card has no action to edit."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-addon-needs-mod","title":"A required game mod may be missing","section":"checking","text":"Players need the Example Pack game mod installed for this to work — say so in your quest description. Where On an addon card, in both the tool-response and pack-data editors. What it means This action depends on the named game mod. A player without that mod may not get the intended result, and the game may not explain what is missing. What to do Name the required game mod in your quest description. The editor cannot install it for the player."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-pack-not-loaded","title":"A pack you cannot re-pick from","section":"checking","text":"The Example Pack pack isn't loaded on this machine. Everything you chose here is saved in the project, so the node keeps working — load the pack again to pick a different data shape. Where In the pack-data editor. What it means Your chosen data and values stay in the project. You can still export them, but the editor cannot offer another data shape until the addon is loaded again. The player's game still needs the related game mod, when the addon requires one. What to do Nothing, unless you want to choose a different shape. Load the addon to change it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-timed-conversation","title":"A conversation that plays live","section":"checking","text":"Timed conversations are sent through the game's live messaging API, one message at a time with the delays you set. Player replies, uploads and “unlocks after” steps only work in the normal declarative script, and the game does not clean live messages up with the quest. Worth an in-game check before you ship it. Where Under Play when the story reaches this node on a dialogue node, once it is switched on. What it means Live playback costs you three things. Replies stop working, uploads stop working, and the messages stay on the player's phone afterwards. What to do Leave it off unless you need the conversation to land on a specific beat. If you need it, test the quest in the game."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-panel-page-unlisted","title":"An unlisted page is missing from search","section":"checking","text":"Not in search results — only a direct URL (or dirhunter) leads here. Where In the website builder, above the preview of an unlisted page. What it means The page is not in in-game search. A direct address, a link or dirhunter can still lead to it; unlisted does not mean private. The editor does not check whether another page links here. What to do Keep it unlisted if that fits your clue, and give the player a way to reach it. If the page should appear in search, turn on Listed in the in-game search ."},{"url":"checking.html#pre-export","title":"The report before you export","section":"checking","text":"Quest or website names can change. Match the rest of the sentence."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-feed-claim","title":"A quest waits for the player to claim it","section":"checking","text":"The player claims this one from its Hackhub feed post — nothing in it runs until they do. Report Good to know — this can be an intentional way to start a quest. What it means The quest does not start on its own. The player must find and claim its feed post first. What to do Leave it this way if the player should claim the job. Turn on Start automatically if it should begin when the mod loads. The only feed post tested in game had text only. It had no poster, employer or comments. If it still fails, open the Quest tab and change Quest identifier . Use one this save has not seen."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-no-start","title":"A quest has no way to start","section":"checking","text":"Nothing can start this quest. It does not start automatically and it is not advertised on the Hackhub feed. Report Red error. What it means The player has no route to claim this quest, and it does not start by itself. What to do Turn on Start automatically , or add a feed post so the player can claim it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-device-target","title":"A port or firewall rule has no target","section":"checking","text":"A “Change port” or “Add firewall rule” node has no device IP, so it has nothing to act on. Report Amber warning. The selected node also shows a field message; see No machine set . What it means The node has no machine to change or protect, so this instruction does nothing. What to do Point it at a machine created by a Create network node. The field message gives the specific choice for that node."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-network-domain","title":"A network claims a domain another server may use","section":"checking","text":"This network claims the domain … Domain names are shared with the whole game. Report Amber warning. What it means The domain is shared by the whole game. Another mod or the base game may already use it. Then its server can answer instead of yours. What to do Choose a distinctive domain connected to your story, rather than a common or placeholder name."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-network-shape","title":"A network the game cannot build","section":"checking","text":"… has machines behind it, but only a router or a splitter can hold other machines — those machines will not be built. Report Red error. What it means A device that cannot contain other devices has one or more machines placed behind it. Those machines will not exist in the exported game. What to do Change the parent device to a router or splitter, or move the child machines under one that can hold them."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-firewall-rules","title":"Firewall rules are on the wrong device","section":"checking","text":"… carries firewall rules, but only a firewall device enforces them — they will be ignored. Report Red error. What it means The rules are saved on a device that is not a firewall, so the game ignores them. What to do Put the rules on a Firewall device between the router and the machine you want to protect."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-login-no-user","title":"A login service has no account to log in as","section":"checking","text":"… has a login service open but no user accounts, so the player cannot break in. Report Red error. What it means The report sees no account you added to this device. It ignores the game's built-in root and guest accounts. The warning may be wrong when those accounts are on. What to do Open the network's Devices . On a Device, check Add stock root and guest accounts ; it is on by default. If it is off, add an account or close the port. This toggle is not on Routers, so add an account there. Test the route in game when stock accounts are on."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-tool-version","title":"A version a tool will not read","section":"checking","text":"… Metasploit needs three numbers (for example “OpenSSH 7.2.0”) — it rejects anything else. Report Red error. What it means The service version number is incomplete or contains a letter. Metasploit expects a three-part number, such as OpenSSH 7.2.0 . The player cannot use that exploit with a different format. What to do Use the version reported by the machine, written as three number parts, such as OpenSSH 7.2.0 ."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-lynx-handle","title":"A Lynx result points to a social profile the game cannot open","section":"checking","text":"The lynx result advertises @… Searching a social handle that has no profile behind it crashes the game and corrupts the player's save. Report Red error. What it means A Tool response for Lynx can contain an @name. The editor cannot create the social profile the game needs to search for it. The warning says that search may crash the game and damage the player's save. What to do Remove the @name from the Lynx result. Or point the player to a website, e-mail address or IP address instead."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-handbook-article","title":"An Open handbook node has no article","section":"checking","text":"An “Open handbook” node has no article, so it does nothing. Report Amber warning. What it means The node has nowhere to take the player. What to do Pick an article only if the node needs to make a handbook call. In HackHub 1.3.1, the game opens the landing page, not your chosen page. See the handbook-entry note and test this in game. Delete the node if you do not need it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-wifi","title":"A note about Create Wi-Fi","section":"checking","text":"“Create Wi-Fi” exports as a native access point in HackHub 1.3.0+. Report Blue information note — not an error. What it means Wi-Fi created here works as a native access point in HackHub 1.3.0 and later. Bettercap may show no network name if you target the access point by its hardware address. Scanning, joining and cracking still worked in that test. What to do No change is needed because of this note. If the displayed name matters to your quest, test that step in the game."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-mail-no-from","title":"A replyable e-mail has no From address","section":"checking","text":"… lets the player reply, but its From address is empty — a player's reply arrives addressed to your mail's From, so with no From there is nothing a trigger can match. Report Blue information note. What it means The player can reply, but the reply has no destination address that your quest can check. What to do Give the e-mail a From address. To react to a reply, add a When event for Mail.Sent . Check that its to field has your address."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-mail-reply","title":"A reply button works, but the reply does not point back to its e-mail","section":"checking","text":"The mail goes out with its reply flag and the Reply button draws. The player's reply arrives addressed to your From address. Report Blue information note. What it means The game shows a Reply button, but the reply contains no reference to the message it answers. The destination is the original e-mail's From address. What to do Use a When event for Mail.Sent to react to a reply. Check whether its to field matches the From address. The event does not identify which e-mail the player answered."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-phone-answer","title":"A phone answer also registers a terminal command","section":"checking","text":"Phone lines with typed answers also register a terminal command (qe-…) the player uses to answer. Report Blue information note. What it means A typed answer in a phone conversation has a command beginning qe- for the player to use. What to do Tell players where to find the command if the phone's answer is part of your quest. Test the call in game before relying on it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-conversation","title":"A conversation set to play live has no wire","section":"checking","text":"… a conversation is set to “play when the story reaches this node” but nothing is wired into it — it stays a normal quest conversation. Report Amber warning. What it means The conversation is set to play at a story beat, but no wire leads into its node. It stays a normal quest conversation instead of playing at that point. What to do Wire the node into the story if it should play at a specific moment. Otherwise change the live-play setting back to a normal quest conversation."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-live-conversation","title":"A live conversation skips normal reply features","section":"checking","text":"A conversation set to “play when the story reaches this node” is sent live at that moment. Player replies, uploads and “unlocks after” steps are skipped. Report Amber warning. What it means Once the story reaches the wired node, the editor sends the conversation live. Replies, file uploads and steps that wait for an earlier message are skipped. The game also does not remove these live messages when the quest ends. What to do Use live playback only if you need the conversation at that exact story moment. Test it in game, especially if you expect replies or uploads."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-kisscord","title":"An upload becomes a text message","section":"checking","text":"Kisscord uploads compile to a “[uploaded file …]” message. Report Blue information note. What it means A Kisscord attachment appears as a line of text, not as a file the player can open. What to do If the player needs the actual file, use Place files and tell them where to find it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-addon","title":"Give data to an addon is not set up","section":"checking","text":"A “Give data to an addon” node is not set up yet — as it stands it does nothing. Report Amber warning. What it means The node has no addon or data shape selected, so it cannot send anything. What to do Open the node and choose its addon pack and data shape. Delete it if you do not need it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-addon-node","title":"An addon action node is not set up","section":"checking","text":"An addon node is not set up yet — add it again from the palette's Editor Mods group. Report Amber warning. What it means The node has no action selected. It will do nothing as it is. What to do If needed, load the pack first. Then remove this card and drag the action into the quest again from the palette's Editor Mods group. Or delete the card if you do not need it."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-addon-mod","title":"A quest needs a game mod for its addon data","section":"checking","text":"Community data is used in this quest. It needs the game mod installed on the player's machine. Report Blue information note. What it means This quest uses community data that depends on a separate game mod. A player without that mod will not get the intended result. What to do Name the required game mod in your quest description so players know they need it. The editor cannot install it for them."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-unlisted-page","title":"An unlisted page will not appear in search","section":"checking","text":"Nothing links to it and the in-game search will not show it. Report Blue information note. What it means The page stays out of in-game search. The note assumes no page links to it, but the editor does not check links. A link, direct address or search with dirhunter can still reach it. Unlisted does not mean private. What to do Leave it unlisted when that fits your clue. Add a link or another way to find it. If the player should find it in search, turn on Listed in the in-game search ."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-duplicate-page-path","title":"Two pages have the same address","section":"checking","text":"… has pages at the path … They ship as two definitions of the same address. Report Red error. What it means Two pages on one website use the same path, so both claim the same address. What to do Give one page a different path. Each page on the site needs its own address."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-page-path","title":"A page path does not start at the website root","section":"checking","text":"The page … has the path …, but paths start at the host root — it should be /…. Report Red error. What it means The in-game browser and dirhunter look for pages from the start of the website address. What to do Start the page path with / , then check the full address in the website preview."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-domain-clash","title":"Two websites on one domain","section":"checking","text":"… is the host of … websites in this mod. Domains are global — two sites on one host will fight over which one answers. Report Red error. What it means Two websites in this mod claim the same host. The report does not inspect other installed mods, so it cannot find every possible conflict. The game may not serve the site you intended. What to do Give each website its own distinctive host name."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-placeholder-domain","title":"A placeholder domain ships as a real website","section":"checking","text":"… is a placeholder domain, but the export ships it as a real site any player can find. Report Amber warning. What it means A sample name such as example.com or localhost will be exported as the actual website address. Another mod may already use it. What to do Choose a distinctive domain name that fits your story."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-handle","title":"A Twotter handle the game will not accept","section":"checking","text":"“@…” cannot be a Twotter handle: use letters, numbers and underscores, 3 to 15 characters. Report Red error. What it means The handle contains a character the game does not accept, or is too short or long. Players search for this handle, so they may never find the profile or clue. What to do Use 3 to 15 letters, numbers or underscores in the handle."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-display","title":"A Twotter account has no display name","section":"checking","text":"@… has no display name. The game shows the display name on the profile and falls back to something generic without it. Report Blue information note. What it means The account still has a handle, but its profile uses a generic name instead of the name you intended. What to do Add a display name if players need to recognise this character. The account can still export without one."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-duplicate","title":"Two Twotter accounts share a handle","section":"checking","text":"Two Twotter accounts share the handle @… Handles are what players search for, so only one of them can ever be found. Report Red error. What it means The game cannot tell which account a player means when both have the same handle. Capital letters do not make two separate handles here. What to do Give one account a different handle."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-account","title":"A Twotter node has no account to post as","section":"checking","text":"… the Twotter node has no account yet. / … points at a Twotter account that is not in this mod any more. Report Red error. What it means The node has no account selected, or the account it used to name has been removed. The post will not appear. What to do Pick an account in the node's Account field. If you need a new character, add it in the Twotter panel under Manage accounts first."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-empty","title":"A Twotter node has nothing to post","section":"checking","text":"… posts nothing — it has no tweet rows. / … has a tweet with no text and no picture. Report Amber warning. What it means The node has no posts, or one post has neither text nor a picture. A blank post is not a useful clue. What to do Add a post and give it text. Or delete the node if you no longer need it. Pictures do not appear in game; see the picture warning ."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-image","title":"A Twotter post picture will not appear in game","section":"checking","text":"The game's posting record has no picture field — the picture stays in your project and in the editor's preview, but players will not see it. Report Amber warning. What it means The editor keeps the picture, but the game does not show it in the timeline or on the post page. This is a game limit, not a broken upload. What to do Put any clue in the post's text, or place it in a file the player can open."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-twotter-time","title":"An older post's time changed when it was opened","section":"checking","text":"… came from an older draft whose tweet time could not be carried over exactly, so it now says “1 month earlier”. Report Blue information note. What it means The editor could not preserve the older post's time exactly while opening the old project. What to do Open the Twotter node and set the amount and time unit to the age you intended."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-export-files-unplaced","title":"Place files could not put its files on a device","section":"checking","text":"A “Place files” node could not be placed — … Point it at a device this quest creates, or target the player's PC instead. Report Amber warning. The files are not in the exported mod where you aimed them. What it means The chosen device is missing, or it lacks an account with a home folder. The project may also lack a network for the device. What to do Point the node at a device this quest creates, add an account if needed, or choose the player's PC. The related field messages explain a missing network , an address with no device and a device with no account ."},{"url":"checking.html#target-matching","title":"Messages about addon targets","section":"checking","text":"The export report shows these amber warnings when your quest uses a loaded addon. That addon must check for target details. Not every addon has these rules. Read your message; the addon decides which services, versions and weaknesses can match."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-target-service","title":"An addon does not recognise a service","section":"checking","text":"“…” on this target means nothing to this addon — its tools only understand …. What it means The target uses a service name this addon's tools do not recognise, so the tool will never match it. What to do Change the service to one named in the warning, or check the addon's own instructions."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-target-version","title":"An addon needs a service version","section":"checking","text":"This target has a service but no version — the addon matches targets by their version string, so a blank version never lines up. What it means The quest listens for an addon that matches by version, but the service has no version filled in. What to do Fill in the version reported by the machine. Do not invent one; it must match what the game tool sees."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-target-no-weakness","title":"An addon is looking for a weakness the quest has not marked","section":"checking","text":"This addon looks for targets with one of these weaknesses. None of this quest's targets names one. What it means The addon searches for the weakness types named in the warning. None of your quest's targets has one. What to do Mark the target with a weakness from the warning's list if that is what the game scenario uses."},{"url":"checking.html#msg-target-weakness-mismatch","title":"An addon does not recognise the marked weakness","section":"checking","text":"This quest's targets are marked …, but this addon only looks for … — none of them lines up. What it means The targets do have weaknesses, but not the type this addon searches for. What to do If the game scenario supports it, choose a weakness from the addon's list in the warning. Otherwise change the addon or quest design. ← How do I… Exporting and installing → ↑ back to top HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-10-03.r267 · message wording and examples on this page are checked against the editor's current source."},{"url":"concepts.html#quest-map","title":"Your quest map","section":"concepts","text":"A project holds three things: quests, websites, and the mod settings that give the whole thing a name. A quest is a map of nodes joined by wires, laid out on a canvas you can pan and zoom. You build that map the way you would sketch one on paper. Drag a node in from the palette. Type into its fields in the inspector. Draw a wire from one of its sockets to a socket on another node. Where the nodes sit changes nothing. Two nodes side by side are not neighbours to the game; only a wire makes them neighbours. The order your story runs in comes from wires, and from nothing else. The canvas invites you to forget this, because a tidy layout looks like a sequence. Some things on the canvas are drawi"},{"url":"concepts.html#lifecycle","title":"When your quest runs","section":"concepts","text":"Four nodes cover the entire life of a quest. They are the only ones the game starts by itself, and nothing runs until one of them does. Quest start Runs once, when the quest begins. Setup goes here: build the network, place the files, tell the player what has happened. On start & reload Runs when the quest is claimed, and again every time the game loads. Anything that must be listening goes here, because a listener set up in Quest start is gone after a reload. On quest complete Runs after the quest is marked complete. A Complete quest node, auto-complete, or the player's Complete button can do that. On quest abandoned Runs if the player gives up. Cleanup goes here. You can use more than one "},{"url":"concepts.html#wire-kinds","title":"Wires have kinds","section":"concepts","text":"There are four kinds of wire. Each one answers a different question, which is why they look different. Wire Colour The question Then Blue What happens next in the story? When Cyan What has to happen in the game? Unlocks Amber, dashed Which objective comes after this one? Data Violet, dotted Where does this value come from? A socket accepts only its own kind of wire. Dragging a flow wire at a condition socket will not connect. That is deliberate rather than fussy. A wire meaning \"and then\" cannot arrive at something that asked \"when\". That would be a question the game has no answer for."},{"url":"concepts.html#objectives","title":"Objectives are how the player knows what to do","section":"concepts","text":"An objective is a line in the player's quest panel. It is the only part of your quest the player is told about. So it is where the story stops being yours and starts being theirs. An objective completes in one of two ways. Either the story flow reaches it, which ticks it off at that moment. Or an event is wired into its Trigger socket. The objective then completes when the player does that thing in the game. The second is the one you want most of the time. It is the difference between \"the story says you did it\" and \"you did it\". Wiring a When event node to an objective's trigger is how a quest notices real play instead of assuming it. Two details the game is strict about. Only the first eve"},{"url":"concepts.html#events","title":"Events are how your quest hears the player","section":"concepts","text":"An event fires when the player does something in the game. Searching for something, logging into a bank, cracking a password, downloading a file. Each of those has a name, and there are 99 of them in 10 groups. A When event node picks one of those names. When it fires, your quest hears about it and carries on down the wire. That single node is how a quest becomes reactive rather than scripted. Some events carry information with them. Terminal.Lynx.Search tells you what the player typed. That is how a quest reacts to a specific search rather than to searching in general. The groups are named after the part of the game they come from. Those are terminal, browser, cracking, files, e-mail, socia"},{"url":"concepts.html#what-export-means","title":"What exporting means","section":"concepts","text":"Your project is the editable thing: the canvas, the fields, the websites. The game cannot play a project. Exporting turns one into a mod, which is a folder the game loads. These two are not interchangeable, and treating them as the same thing is how work gets lost. The mod is finished output. You cannot open it again and carry on building. Everything about the file it produces, where it goes and what to do about updates. All of it is on the exporting and installing page."},{"url":"concepts.html#permissions","title":"Permissions, in plain words","section":"concepts","text":"A permission is a promise about what your mod is allowed to touch. The game shows the list to the player when they install, so it is a promise the player reads. You never pick permissions. The editor works out which ones your nodes need, from seven in total, and asks for exactly those. Delete the node and the permission disappears with it. Permission In plain words network The mod puts machines, names and files into the game's world. filesystem The mod writes files onto the player's own machine. shell The mod runs commands, answers for the game's tools, or adds a command of its own. events The mod watches for what the player does, or starts another quest. mail The mod sends messages to the p"},{"url":"concepts.html#why-no-complete","title":"How quests finish","section":"concepts","text":"A quest finishes when something marks it complete. The clearest way is the Complete quest node, placed at the end of a success path. You can also turn on autoComplete . Then the game closes the quest when every objective is done. Use that when the objectives alone prove success. On quest complete is not the ending itself. It is the path that runs after one of those finish routes fires. So keep critical clues before the finish point. Keep one deliberate finish path. A final objective, a closing message and then Complete quest is the easiest shape to read. Check it in a dry run before export. ← Your first quest Node reference → HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-10-03."},{"url":"export.html#export-dialog","title":"The export window","section":"export","text":"Opening the export window shows you what you are about to ship before it is packed. Its own words for the job: Compiles everything into a ready-to-play mod folder — no coding needed. Unzip it into the game's mods/ directory. Quests Every quest in the project, by title. If a quest is missing from this list, it is not in the mod either. Websites Every host your mod registers. Worth a look, because domains belong to the whole game and two mods claiming one host will fight over it. Permissions requested The list below. The game shows these to the player when they install, so a shorter list is a friendlier one. In the zip Every file that will be packed, by path. Nothing is hidden from you here. N"},{"url":"export.html#permissions","title":"The seven permissions","section":"export","text":"A permission is a promise about what your mod touches. You do not pick them anywhere: the editor works out which ones your nodes need and asks for exactly those. Delete the node and the permission goes with it. Permission Asked for by What it lets the mod do network Create network · Register domain · Create database · Place files Put machines, names and files into the game's world. filesystem Place files Write files onto the player's own machine. shell Run terminal command · Tool response · Manual input Run commands, answer for the game's tools, and add a command of your own. events When event · Claim another quest Watch for what the player does, and start another quest. mail Dialogue, with "},{"url":"export.html#zip-contents","title":"What is in the .zip","section":"export","text":"Two of these files are the mod. The rest let somebody who wants to change the mod later rebuild it. They also make the folder make sense to anyone who opens it. You never need to touch any of them. File What it is dist/mod.js The mod. Everything you built, turned into something the game can run. manifest.json The mod's card: its name, author, description, version and the permissions it asks for. This is what the game reads first. dist/manifest.json A second copy of the card, where the game also looks for it. README.md A short description of the mod, with its permissions and any warnings the export produced. src/index.ts The same mod as dist/mod.js , in the form a developer would edit. packag"},{"url":"export.html#conditional-outputs","title":"Files that appear only when used","section":"export","text":"The archive can also include extra pictures and widget pages. These are added only when your project contains the matching item. Their names are made for you; leave them where they are if you unzip the mod. Mod icon. A supported PNG or JPEG mod icon is copied to assets/icon.png or assets/icon.jpg . Mod cover. A supported PNG or JPEG cover image is copied to assets/cover.png or assets/cover.jpg . Supported PNG or JPEG pictures go into assets/ with generated names. This includes quest icons, employer pictures, feed posts and comments. The picture stays in your project too. Desktop widgets. Each widget adds its own .html page under widgets/ . The editor links it to the widget for you. No matchi"},{"url":"export.html#installing","title":"Installing it","section":"export","text":"The file downloads with a name built from your mod's identifier and version. So meridian-1.0.0.zip is version 1.0.0 of the mod called meridian . Two versions of one mod therefore never share a filename. That matters when you are testing a change against the last one. Open the export window and read the report. Fix anything red. Choose Download .zip . The button reads Packing… while the file is built. Unzip the folder into the game's mods/ directory, as the export window tells you. The folder inside the archive is the mod; the archive itself is not. Start the game. The mod is loaded, and its quests are available to accept. Unzip it, do not drop it in whole. The game reads a folder in mods/ , "},{"url":"export.html#export-is-not-a-save","title":"Export is not a save","section":"export","text":"This is the mistake worth understanding properly, because it loses work. The .zip is the finished mod. It is the thing the game plays. It is not something you can open in the editor and carry on editing. Your project file is the editable thing. It holds your canvas, your node settings and your websites in the form the editor understands. Saving keeps that up to date; exporting only produces a copy for the game to run. To keep working Save the project, and open the project again next time. Not the .zip . To ship a change Edit the project, then export again. The new .zip replaces the old folder in mods/ . If you only have the .zip The quests inside it still play, but the canvas you built them "},{"url":"export.html#build-stamp","title":"The build stamp","section":"export","text":"Every export writes the editor's own build into dist/mod.js , as 2026-10-03.r267 . It is how you tell which version of the editor produced a mod. That is the first thing worth knowing when a quest behaves differently for someone else than it does for you. The same stamp is in the footer of every page of this handbook. So you can check that the handbook you are reading describes the editor you are using. A handbook for an older build can describe a field that has since moved. When you report a problem, give the build. Open any .zip you exported and the stamp is at the top of dist/mod.js . It saves everyone a round of questions. ← Checking your quest When something goes wrong → ↑ back to top H"},{"url":"guides.html#inspector-tabs","title":"The inspector's three tabs","section":"guides","text":"The panel on the right holds three tabs, and one of them is disabled until you need it. Node Every field on the node you have selected. Greyed out when nothing is selected, because there is nothing to show. Quest Settings for the quest you are looking at. This is the default tab, so it is what you see on an empty canvas. Mod Settings for the whole file: the name on the box, the version, the icon. The inspector at rest. Node waits for a selection. The panel can also leave the side of the window. Drag its left edge outward and it floats, which helps when a node sits underneath it. Drag the title bar to move it and the bottom-right corner to resize it. The same panel, floating. Its position is "},{"url":"guides.html#quest-settings","title":"Quest settings","section":"guides","text":"Six groups. Most quests need four of them. Identity Quest identifier Internal. Unique across your mod, and the thing other quests name when they start this one. Display title What the player sees in their quest list. Description The pitch shown before the player accepts. Use it to name any game mod the quest needs. Journal group Folders the player's journal into groups. Two quests in the same group read as one story. Hackhub feed post The toggle that puts the quest on the Hackhub feed as a job the player can accept on the spot. It is the organic way to be found. The scripted Claim another quest node covers quests the story starts itself. Quests accepted from the feed complete from the post i"},{"url":"guides.html#mod-settings","title":"Mod settings","section":"guides","text":"These describe the file, not the story. Everything here ends up on the card the game shows the player. Mod id Internal, and part of the exported file's name. Changing it later makes the game treat the result as a different mod. Display name The name on the box. Version Also part of the filename, which is how two builds of one mod avoid overwriting each other. Author Your name. Defaults to the editor's name if you leave it. Description The blurb in the mod list. Cover image and Icon — the two pictures. Both optional, both shipped inside the .zip . Tags Search terms for the mod list. The Mod tab. Permissions appear here but are not editable — your nodes choose them."},{"url":"guides.html#dialogues","title":"Dialogues","section":"guides","text":"The Dialogues button opens the phone conversations, in one place rather than scattered across the canvas. This is where you write who says what, in what order, and with what delay. One conversation, line by line. If nothing leads there, the canvas can flag the node as unreachable. Do not rely on the export report to catch it."},{"url":"guides.html#websites","title":"Websites","section":"guides","text":"The Websites button opens the builder. Your sites are on the left, and the pages of whichever site you picked on the right. A site is a host; a page is a path on it. Sites and their pages. Each page has a live preview beside the editor, so you see roughly what the player will see. Editing one page, preview alongside. Web addresses can overlap. Two sites in one mod that use the same host can compete over the same address. The export report checks for duplicates in this mod, but cannot inspect other installed mods. Give each site its own distinctive host. A page can also be unlisted. It stays out of game search. A player may still reach it by address, a link or a brute-force tool. Unlisted doe"},{"url":"guides.html#templates","title":"Templates","section":"guides","text":"The picker has fourteen entries: twelve templates and two Reference sheets. Each card shows difficulty and node count. The window also offers Export current quest and Import a quest file . The template list. Loading a template replaces the current project and clears undo history. Autosave is not a versioned backup, so save a project file before experimenting. Here is every card in the picker. The summaries name the kind of work each one teaches, not its plot. The first twelve are starter quests; the last two are lookup sheets, not quests to edit. Blank quest — Beginner · 4 nodes. Start with the basic quest entry points and build everything else yourself. First Contact — Beginner · 8 nodes. A"},{"url":"guides.html#dry-run","title":"Dry run","section":"guides","text":"The Dry run button walks through your quest the way the exported mod would run it, without installing anything. It is the closest thing to a test drive the editor has. It runs the editor's emitted mod against a small recording version of the game's tools. A trace in progress. Nodes light up as the run reaches them. Watch the order and which path the run takes. A missing node may have no wire, or this run may have taken another branch. Read the results carefully Opening Dry run starts a run. After you edit the quest, choose Run again . The dialog lists the story steps it reached, then shows how each objective completed or what its event check found. would tick . The listener matched a sample "},{"url":"guides.html#addons","title":"Addons","section":"guides","text":"Addons are packs made by other people that add events and tool actions to your editor. The window's own description of them: Community addons drop in here as data: their events join the trigger picker, their quest data becomes nodes. Addons are data only — the editor never runs code from them. The addons window. Drag a pack in, and its events and nodes appear. Once a pack is loaded, its events show up in the trigger picker under Community addons . Its actions appear in a palette group that starts with Editor Mods and ends with the pack name. Nothing changes until you load one. Your players need the related game mod too. Features that depend on an addon may not work for players who have not i"},{"url":"guides.html#settings","title":"Settings","section":"guides","text":"The Settings button holds editor preferences. None of them reach your quest or the exported mod; they change how the editor looks and feels for you. Canvas Show grid , Grid colour , Grid opacity , Grid scale and Snap to grid . Wires Animated wires , Springy wires , Line weight and Mark size . Appearance Interface font , with a Follow the editor theme option for the grid. Getting out Clear the autosaved draft and Reset all editor preferences . Both are deliberate and both ask first. Preferences only. Your quest is untouched by anything here."},{"url":"guides.html#canvas-tools","title":"Canvas tools","section":"guides","text":"Top left of the canvas. These move nodes around; none of them change what your quest does. Tidy up Arranges the whole graph left to right, by how far each node sits from its entry point. The one to reach for after a long building session. Align in a row · Align in a column Puts the selected nodes on one line, centred. Space out across · Space out down Equalises the gaps without moving anything onto a line. The outermost nodes stay where they are. Springy wires Switches the wires between settling and drifting. Purely cosmetic, and off is calmer on a big map. Debug Opens the editor's own diagnostics: the build stamp, wire numbers, counters and a recent-event log. This is not the Debug probe no"},{"url":"guides.html#pack-extras","title":"Things outside your quests","section":"guides","text":"This section covers three things a pack can add outside a quest. They are a start-menu entry, a small box on the desktop and right-click entries. They all live in one window, opened from Extras on the toolbar. Nothing there is required: a pack with none of it exports exactly as it always did."},{"url":"guides.html#menu-entry","title":"A start-menu entry","section":"guides","text":"The start menu has a strip of entries along the bottom. A pack can add one, with a picture. It has four possible jobs. It can say something, start one of your quests, send the player a letter, or open the game's own handbook. Picture Optional, and worth having for a pack whose entry sits beside the game's own. Pick a picture from your machine. It travels inside the pack, so keep it small. The menu draws it the size of a letter in this sentence. The entry's own words The label, and the sentence a \"say something\" entry shows. They appear as you write them; the editor has no screen for managing translated text yet. When it appears As soon as the player's game loads your pack. Nothing has to be "},{"url":"guides.html#desktop-widget","title":"A box on the desktop","section":"guides","text":"A widget is a small web page the game draws on the player's desktop. Think a note, a counter, a status board. You build it with the same editor the website builder uses, including the preview. Size and place Width, height and the corner it sits at, in the desktop's own pixels. See-through Off by default, so a widget arrives with a background. Turn it on for a widget meant to look painted onto the wallpaper. A widget's own words are a page, not a quest. What you type inside the widget is shown as you typed it: translated words do not reach in there, because the widget is a file the game opens, not a line of text it fills in. Write those words in the language you want them in."},{"url":"guides.html#right-click-entry","title":"An entry in a right-click menu","section":"guides","text":"Where a player right-clicks, a pack can add an entry. That can be on a file, on the desktop, on the taskbar, or on a window. Each entry carries a label, an optional picture, and one of the four jobs above. Use it for the thing a player reaches for while looking at something. Think \"inspect this file\", \"send this to my notes\", or \"open the walkthrough\"."},{"url":"guides.html#translated-words","title":"Translated text is not available yet","section":"guides","text":"The game can show translated text, but this editor does not currently offer a screen for writing or checking translations. Text you enter in quests and pack extras is used as written; changing the game's language does not translate it. This handbook does not give steps for a feature the editor cannot currently manage."},{"url":"guides.html#extras-handbook","title":"The handbook entry, honestly","section":"guides","text":"One of the four jobs opens the game's handbook. In HackHub 1.3.1, the book opens on its own landing page; it does not jump to the page you chose. The entry can still point players toward the handbook. The editor's log records which page was requested. See When something goes wrong for help finding the log."},{"url":"guides.html#generate-tags","title":"Two buttons beside a text field","section":"guides","text":"Some text fields carry one or two small buttons. They look alike and do opposite things, which is worth getting straight early. The dice Fills the field with a sample value, such as an address, a contractor's name or an e-mail. It becomes ordinary text in your project. It stays until you edit it or generate another value. The sparkle It may stand for a saved answer, player detail or generated value. The tag tells the game which one. Roll the dice when you want something concrete to look at while you build. Use the sparkle when the game is the only thing that knows the answer. A machine address is the common case, since the game allocates it. Both are ordinary edits. Either one can be undone "},{"url":"how-do-i.html","title":"How do I… — HackHub Quest Editor handbook How do I… Walkthroughs for practical t","section":"how-do-i.html","text":"How do I… — HackHub Quest Editor handbook How do I… Walkthroughs for practical tasks authors handle in the editor. Each guide solves one job with clear, grounded steps. All 17 guides are written and linked below. These are practical arrangements built from editor features. Each guide names the nodes you need and estimates the time. It explains why the setup works and lists common failure points. Examples draw on built-in templates and the game's own quests. Walkthrough What it gets you Give the player an objective, and make it tick The shape almost every quest is built on. Send the player a me"},{"url":"index.html#contents-learn","title":"Learn it once","section":"index","text":"Your first quest Twenty minutes, start to finish, ending in a mod you install and play. How a quest works The mental model in eight short pieces the rest of the book keeps referring back to."},{"url":"index.html#contents-look","title":"Look things up","section":"index","text":"Node reference Each node has a page for its settings, defaults, and limits. It explains what happens when you leave a setting empty. Pages follow the palette order: Quest lifecycle Quest start · On start & reload · On quest complete · On quest abandoned Objectives Objective Triggers When event World building Create network · Create Wi-Fi · Firewall rule · Change port · Register domain · Create database · Place files · Tool response Communication Dialogue Custom terminal Manual input Effects Pay or charge the player, message them, change quest status, open the handbook, run a terminal command, and more. Community addons Give data to an addon · Addon node Flow control Branch · Wait · Reroute ·"},{"url":"index.html#contents-fix","title":"When it goes sideways","section":"index","text":"Checking your quest Find every message the editor shows about a problem. Browse messages from the canvas, inspector, panels, and export report. Paste exact text to search for a fix. When something goes wrong A decision tree that starts from the symptom, not the cause. Exporting and installing See what the export window checks and what goes in your .zip. Learn where to install the mod, and why export does not save your project. Glossary and limits Every word, every key, and everything this editor cannot do yet. Three things worth knowing before you start A quest finishes when your graph says so. Put Complete quest at a success ending, or use Behaviour when objectives alone should finish it. Y"},{"url":"nodes/comms-dialogue.html","title":"Dialogue — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Dialogue Communication comms.dialogue W","section":"nodes/comms-dialogue.html","text":"Dialogue — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Dialogue Communication comms.dialogue What it does A conversation between the player and a character: a phone call, a Kisscord chat, an e-mail or a WeeChat session. When you'd use it Use it for anything a character says to the player. Pick the form the story wants: a call for urgency, mail for a paper trail. For phone calls, choose when Out fires : when the call hangs up, or right after it starts. The second output is there for conversations that can go badly. Wire it if the player can answer wrongly. In the game, this looks like The conversation appear"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-accountId","title":"Account","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Which of the mod's Twotter accounts posts these. Accounts are shared by every quest, so one character can carry a story arc across several of them — make one with “Manage accounts”. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-tweets","title":"Tweets","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"One row is a single post. A handful of rows with “Already on the profile” times is a history the player finds when they look the character up — a month of excitement before a disappearance, say. Default a starter list The button reads Add a tweet ."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-content","title":"Tweet","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"What the character wrote. Tags work here, like everywhere else an author writes text."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-timeMode","title":"When","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"“When the story arrives” posts the tweet as the flow reaches this node. “Already on the profile” has it sitting there from the start, that far back. Limits One of the 2 listed below. Choice What it is stored as When the story arrives arrival Already on the profile earlier"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-agoAmount","title":"How long ago","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Counted back from the moment the story reaches this node, on the in-game clock. Limits 1 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-agoUnit","title":"Unit","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Minutes through years, the same units a Timer takes. Limits One of the 6 listed below. Choice What it is stored as minutes minutes hours hours days days weeks weeks months months years years"},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-likes","title":"Likes","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Cosmetic, but it sells the fiction. A brand-new account with 400 likes reads as fake. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-comments","title":"Replies","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many replies the post shows. The game does not open them — this is the number under the post, nothing more. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-shares","title":"Reposts","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many times the post was shared. Zero is fine and often truest — a small account screaming into the void. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-views","title":"Views","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"How many people saw it. It has to look plausible next to the likes: thousands of views with two likes reads wrong. Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/comms-tweet.html#node-comms-tweet-field-showInTimeline","title":"Also in the main timeline","section":"nodes/comms-tweet","text":"Off keeps a year of history on the profile where the player looked for it. On drops it into the public feed too — good for an announcement the whole city should see. Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the tweets are posted. Out output Whatever happens after the posts go out. The node does not wait for the player to read anything. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by"},{"url":"nodes/entry-abandon.html","title":"On quest abandoned — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest abandoned Quest life","section":"nodes/entry-abandon.html","text":"On quest abandoned — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest abandoned Quest lifecycle entry.abandon What it does Runs if the player abandons the quest. Use it to tidy up whatever the quest left behind. When you'd use it Use it when the quest leaves something behind that should be undone if the player abandons it. For example, a machine left unlocked or a contact left waiting mid-conversation. Most quests do not need it. If nothing has to be undone, delete the node rather than leaving it sitting there empty. In the game, this looks like The player drops the quest and this fires. Anything you wi"},{"url":"nodes/entry-complete.html","title":"On quest complete — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest complete Quest lifecy","section":"nodes/entry-complete.html","text":"On quest complete — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On quest complete Quest lifecycle entry.complete What it does Runs when the quest is marked complete. That can come from a Complete quest node, auto-complete, or the player's Complete button. When you'd use it Use it for cleanup or epilogue beats that should happen after the quest is finished. For a deliberate ending, place Complete quest where the story should finish. Anything wired here runs after that finish point, not before it. In the game, this looks like The player sees the quest finish, then this path runs through the game's quest-compl"},{"url":"nodes/entry-load.html","title":"On start & reload — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On start & reload Quest lifecy","section":"nodes/entry-load.html","text":"On start & reload — HackHub Quest Editor handbook On start & reload Quest lifecycle entry.load What it does Runs when the quest is claimed, and again every time the game loads it. Anything that has to be true in every session goes here. When you'd use it Put anything that watches for a player action here rather than in Quest start . A watcher armed once stops working after the player reloads. Use it for the pieces a returning player expects to still be working. Examples include the triggers waiting for the player and the state of a machine they left mid-job. Keep one-off story beats out of it."},{"url":"nodes/entry-start.html","title":"Quest start — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Quest start Quest lifecycle entry.st","section":"nodes/entry-start.html","text":"Quest start — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Quest start Quest lifecycle entry.start What it does Runs the moment the quest is claimed. Anything the story needs in place before the player acts goes here. When you'd use it This is the front door of your quest. Wire the opening to its Out socket: the briefing mail, the first objective, whatever the player should meet first. You can have more than one, and the game runs all of them when the quest is claimed. Splitting a long opening across two is fine. It has no input socket, because nothing runs before it. In the game, this looks like The player "},{"url":"nodes/flow-appcheck.html#node-flow-appcheck-field-app","title":"App name","section":"nodes/flow-appcheck","text":"Pick an app the desktop knows, or type another exactly as the desktop spells it — a spelling it does not use never matches. Terminal commands cannot be checked here; the editor says so if you type one. What to put here Pick one from the list — those are the apps the desktop reports, so a pick can never be misspelled. If yours is not there, type it and make it match the desktop exactly, capitalization included. The editor warns you if you type a terminal command, because those can never be checked here. Default empty Limits One of the 9 listed below. If you leave it empty With no name there is nothing to look for, so the Missing path always runs. The editor warns you while you are still looki"},{"url":"nodes/flow-appcheck.html#node-flow-appcheck-field-saveList","title":"Also save the list of installed apps","section":"nodes/flow-appcheck","text":"Stores every app the player has, so a later Branch can test the list or a message can mention what they actually own. Also the quickest way to see which apps the game counts on your own save — send the list to a Notify and read it in game. What to put here Stores every app the player has under the name below, then carries on with the check as normal. The node does both. Default on Limits On or off. Watch out It is on by default. Keeping the list costs nothing. It is the only way to see which apps the game counts. Turn it off if no part of the quest reads it."},{"url":"nodes/flow-appcheck.html#node-flow-appcheck-field-key","title":"Save the list as","section":"nodes/flow-appcheck","text":"The name the list is stored under. Two checks using the same name refresh it, which is what you want after the player unlocks something. What to put here Anything you like. Other parts of the quest use this label to find the list again. The player never sees it. Default \"installedApps\" Watch out Two checks using the same name refresh it, which is what you want after the player unlocks something. Use two different names if you want to keep a snapshot of each moment. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the check. Trigger input The event that wakes the check, when it should react to something the player does. Installed output The path that runs"},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-title","title":"Headline","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"A short name for this beat, e.g. “Act 2 — the firewall”. It is the card's title bar. What to put here The card's title bar. Name the beat after the scene. Example Act 2 — the firewall Default empty If you leave it empty The card has no title, so on a crowded canvas you cannot tell one beat from another."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-text","title":"Beat text","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"The key content of the beat. The card previews the top ~800 characters and expands into a scrollable panel for the rest. What to put here What happens in the beat. The card previews the top of it and expands in the inspector. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-choices","title":"Branch choices","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Add a row for each path the story can take. Each gets its own output socket on the card, so you can wire each branch onward while planning. Default an empty list The button reads Add choice ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-label","title":"Choice","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Short title for this path, e.g. “Go in through the front door”. Example Go in through the front door"},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-note","title":"What happens","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"One line on where this path leads, so a branching beat reads at a glance."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-width","title":"Width (px)","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"How wide the card is on the canvas, in pixels. Default 280 Limits 200 to 640. Whole numbers, in steps of 20."},{"url":"nodes/flow-beat.html#node-flow-beat-field-color","title":"Card colour","section":"nodes/flow-beat","text":"Pick any colour. Colour-coding by act, character or branch is usually the clearest. What to put here Colour the card by act, character or branch, so a big canvas reads at a glance. Default \"#64748b\" Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Story Beat . Common mistakes Expecting"},{"url":"nodes/flow-branch.html#node-flow-branch-field-source","title":"Test against","section":"nodes/flow-branch","text":"Test against details from the event that fired, or against quest data you stored earlier with a “Set quest data” node. What to put here Choose based on where the information comes from. Pick Details from the event for values sent with the game event. Pick Quest data for values saved earlier using Set quest data . Default \"event\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Pick Quest data when the thing you are testing happened earlier in the quest. The event that woke this branch only carries its own details. Choice What it is stored as Details from the event event Quest data data"},{"url":"nodes/flow-branch.html#node-flow-branch-field-conditions","title":"Take the “Yes” path when","section":"nodes/flow-branch","text":"All clauses must hold for the “Yes” path. Otherwise the “No” path runs. What to put here Add one clause per thing that has to be true. Default an empty list If you leave it empty With no clauses, there is nothing to fail. The Yes path always runs, and the No path never does. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the split. Trigger input The event that wakes the branch, when it should react to something the player does. Yes output The path that runs when every clause holds. No output The path that runs when any clause fails. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of th"},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-label","title":"Label","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Names itself after whatever you wire it to — socket, node and detail. Type your own to override it. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-includeData","title":"Include saved values","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Print everything the quest has saved with a “Remember a value” node. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-includePayload","title":"Include the event","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Also print the details the game passed to this point, so you can see the real values instead of guessing what arrived. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/flow-debug.html#node-flow-debug-field-toast","title":"Show on screen too","section":"nodes/flow-debug","text":"Also show it on screen, so you can test without reading the log file. Default off Limits On or off. Watch out Switch it off before you ship. Debug output a player can see shows them the seams. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find Debug probe under "},{"url":"nodes/flow-delay.html#node-flow-delay-field-seconds","title":"Seconds","section":"nodes/flow-delay","text":"How long the story pauses here. Fractions are fine — 0.5 waits half a second. Example 2 Default 1 Limits 0 or more. Steps of 0.5, so fractions that fine are allowed. If you leave it empty There is no pause. A blank value counts as zero and the story carries straight on. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them a"},{"url":"nodes/flow-note.html#node-flow-note-field-text","title":"Note","section":"nodes/flow-note","text":"Shown on the canvas only. Notes are never exported into the mod. Default empty"},{"url":"nodes/flow-note.html#node-flow-note-field-width","title":"Width","section":"nodes/flow-note","text":"How wide the note is on the canvas, in pixels. Default 240 Limits 160 to 640. Whole numbers, in steps of 20. Wires This node has no sockets of its own. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Sticky note . Related nodes Group frame — A named box that moves its nodes. Story Beat — Plans a story beat. Never runs. ← Debug probe Story Beat → ↑ back to top HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-10-03.r267 · fiel"},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-options","title":"Options","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"One of these is picked at random and stored. Add as many as you like. What to put here The values to choose between. Add as many as you like. Default an empty list Watch out With one option there is nothing random about it. With none, there is nothing to pick. The button reads Add option ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-label","title":"Value","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"The value stored if this option is picked."},{"url":"nodes/flow-random.html#node-flow-random-field-storeAs","title":"Store the result as","section":"nodes/flow-random","text":"A name you choose. The picked value is saved under it so you can read it back later with {{data.name}}. Example guardOnDuty If you leave it empty The pick is not stored, so nothing can read it back later. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Random pick . Common mistakes On"},{"url":"nodes/flow-reroute.html","title":"Reroute — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Reroute Flow control flow.reroute What i","section":"nodes/flow-reroute.html","text":"Reroute — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Reroute Flow control flow.reroute What it does A point to route wires through. It tidies the canvas and changes nothing about the story. When you'd use it Use it when one node has to feed several others, or when a wire would otherwise cross half the canvas. Double-click any wire on the canvas to drop one in automatically. In the game, this looks like Nothing. It changes how the canvas looks, not how the quest runs. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in the story and runs whatever is wired after it. Wires Socket Direction What to"},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-steps","title":"Outputs, in order","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"They fire top to bottom. Drag the rows' ✕ to remove an output — any wire attached to it is removed too. What to put here One row per output, in the order they should fire. Default a starter list Watch out Removing a row removes its socket, and any wire attached to it goes with it. The button reads Add output ."},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-label","title":"Name","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"Free text — whatever helps you recognise this output on the canvas, e.g. “lights out” or “call Mara”. What to put here A name for the output, so you can tell the sockets apart on the canvas."},{"url":"nodes/flow-sequence.html#node-flow-sequence-field-delayMs","title":"Wait before firing (milliseconds)","section":"nodes/flow-sequence","text":"How long to pause before this output fires, counted from the previous one. 0 fires it immediately; 1000 is one second. Example 1500 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 100. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Sequence . Common mistakes A row is deleted without checking its wire. The socket goes with it and whatever hun"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-mode","title":"When it fires","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"A stretch to wait, a clock time counted from now, or a fixed moment in the story's own calendar. Default \"after\" Limits One of the 3 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Wait after A coming day daytime An exact date at"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-years","title":"Years","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"Whole in-game years to wait, before the shorter units beside it. A target month with fewer days uses its last day. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. It adds one in-game year to the calendar. The day number stays the same. If the next year has no leap day, 29 February becomes 28 February."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-months","title":"Months","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"Whole in-game months to wait, on top of the years. 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not in March. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. It adds one in-game month to the calendar. For example, 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not March, because February has no 31st."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-weeks","title":"Weeks","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game weeks to wait, on top of the months. One week is seven in-game days. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. A week is seven in-game days, so 2 weeks and 14 days wait exactly the same length of time."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-days","title":"Days","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game days to wait first. 0 is fine — the units are added up. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. The units are added together, so days, months and hours count towards one due time. If every box is blank, the timer does not wait."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-hours","title":"Hours","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game hours, on top of the days. 25 hours is a legal value. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. The units are added up, so 25 hours is a legal value."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-minutes","title":"Minutes","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game minutes, on top of the days and hours. At the default game speed one in-game minute passes every real second. Example 30 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Counts as zero. These are in-game minutes. At the default game speed, one in-game minute passes every real second, so 30 minutes is half a real minute."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetYears","title":"Years","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game years from now, counted from the day the story reaches this node. A target month with fewer days uses its last day. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no years. Count from the in-game day the player reaches this node. So “1 year, at 18:23” is that same date next year. If the next year has no leap day, 29 February becomes 28 February."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetMonths","title":"Months","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game months from now, on top of the years. 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, not in March. Example 1 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no months. Count whole in-game months from the day the player gets here. So 31 January plus one month lands on 28 February, rather than skipping into March."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetWeeks","title":"Weeks","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game weeks from now, on top of the months. One week is seven in-game days. Example 2 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means no weeks. A week is seven in-game days, so 2 weeks is 14 days. Use the clock time below to set when the timer rings, whatever time the player arrived."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-offsetDays","title":"Days","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"In-game days from now, on top of the weeks and months. 0 means today, so “0 days, at 09:00” fires the next time the clock reads 09:00. Example 3 Default 0 Limits 0 or more. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 means today — the next time the clock reads the time below. Days are added after the months and weeks. With all offset fields at 0, the timer can still ring today at 09:00."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateDay","title":"Day","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game day of the month, as the in-game clock shows it. The field warns when that day does not exist. Example 20 Default 0 Limits 1 to 31. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 1–31, as the in-game clock shows it."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateMonth","title":"Month","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game month, by name, as the in-game clock shows it. Example 9 Default 0 Limits One of the 13 listed below. If you leave it empty 1–12, as the in-game clock shows it. January is 1. Choice What it is stored as Not set yet 0 January 1 February 2 March 3 April 4 May 5 June 6 July 7 August 8 September 9 October 10 November 11 December 12"},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-dateYear","title":"Year","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The in-game year, e.g. 2026. Read it off the in-game clock, not your own calendar. Example 2026 Default 0 Limits 1970 to 9999. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty Read it off the in-game clock, not your own calendar. With no full date set, the timer fires the moment the story reaches it."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-hour","title":"Hour","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The hour the in-game clock will show, 0–23. This is the player's clock, not the wall clock. Example 12 Default 0 Limits 0 to 23. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 is legal — it is 00:00, the middle of the in-game night. The time the in-game clock will show, not the wall clock."},{"url":"nodes/flow-timer.html#node-flow-timer-field-minute","title":"Minute","section":"nodes/flow-timer","text":"The minute the in-game clock will show, 0–59. Example 0 Default 0 Limits 0 to 59. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty 0 is legal — on the hour. In-game seconds are real-world milliseconds, so there is no second field. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Tim"},{"url":"nodes/fx-claimquest.html#node-fx-claimquest-field-questName","title":"Quest","section":"nodes/fx-claimquest","text":"The identifier of the quest to start next. It must exist in this mod or another installed one. What to put here The identifier of the quest to start next, exactly as that quest names itself. Example meridian-act-two Default empty If you leave it empty There is no quest to claim, so the node does nothing. Watch out The identifier has to match. A name that exists nowhere claims nothing, and the player is left with a finished story and no next chapter. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire"},{"url":"nodes/fx-completequest.html","title":"Complete quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Complete quest Effects fx.complet","section":"nodes/fx-completequest.html","text":"Complete quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Complete quest Effects fx.completeQuest What it does Marks this quest complete. Rewards can pay out, and On quest complete runs next. When you'd use it Use it as the final node of a successful story path. Put closing mail, payments and important clues before it, unless they belong under On quest complete . It has no output because the current story path ends here. In the game, this looks like The quest leaves the active list as completed. Quest rewards and complete-hook work can run. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in "},{"url":"nodes/fx-handbook.html#node-fx-handbook-field-articleId","title":"Article","section":"nodes/fx-handbook","text":"The page to open. In game 1.3.1 this opens the handbook on its own landing page: reaching a particular page is not possible until the page names the game uses are known (see the manual). Watch out An identifier that matches no article opens nothing, and the player gets no explanation of why."},{"url":"nodes/fx-handbook.html#node-fx-handbook-field-category","title":"Category","section":"nodes/fx-handbook","text":"The handbook section the article sits under. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Open handbook . Common mistakes The article belongs to another mod. The player does not have it, so the handbook opens on nothing. Point at an article your own mod ships. Messages you might se"},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-message","title":"Message","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"The text on the popup. Type {{data.name}} to insert a value you saved earlier. What to put here One or two sentences is plenty. It sits over the game, not in a journal. Example Payment received: Meridian Capital. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The popup appears with nothing in it. The player sees an empty box and no idea why."},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-variant","title":"Style","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"A notification is a persistent popup; a toast slides in and fades on its own. What to put here Pick how insistent the message should be. Default \"notify\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out A notification waits for the player to close it. Use a toast for anything they can afford to miss. Choice What it is stored as Notification popup notify Toast toast"},{"url":"nodes/fx-notify.html#node-fx-notify-field-tone","title":"Tone","section":"nodes/fx-notify","text":"Sets the colour and icon: info, success, warning or error. What to put here Match it to what the message means: a success for a payment, a warning for a mistake. Default \"info\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Info info Success success Warning warning Error error Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection "},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-amount","title":"Amount","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"Dollars deposited into the player's bank account. What to put here The dollars to award when this node runs. Example 250 Default 100 Limits 0 or more. If you leave it empty A zero payout still runs, but the player receives nothing."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-description","title":"Description","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The label on the bank statement line. What to put here Write how the client names the job when the transfer appears. Example Meridian Capital — contract settlement Default \"Job payment\" If you leave it empty The money arrives with no useful label, so the reward looks unexplained."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-fromIBAN","title":"From IBAN","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The sending account, shown in the transfer details. What to put here Use this when a client, shell company or agency should appear as the sender. Example BG80BNBG96611020345678 If you leave it empty The game still creates a transfer. Fill it when the sender matters to the fiction."},{"url":"nodes/fx-pay.html#node-fx-pay-field-fromName","title":"From name","section":"nodes/fx-pay","text":"The sender's name on the statement. What to put here Name the sender shown in the transfer details. Example Meridian Capital If you leave it empty The payment still arrives. Fill it when the player should recognise who paid them. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever moment earns the money. Out output The closing message, next objective, or follow-up effect after the payment lands. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step "},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-title","title":"Title","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The heading at the top of the question box. Leave blank if the question itself is enough. Example Security check Default \"Answer needed\" Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The box can still read well if the question says enough on its own."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-label","title":"Question","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The sentence above the answer box — what you want the player to answer. Example Enter the recovery code: Default \"Type your answer:\" Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The player sees an input box with little context. Usually this should ask the actual question."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-placeholder","title":"Example text","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Grey hint inside an empty answer box. It disappears as soon as the player types. What to put here A sample shape helps when the answer has dashes, spaces or a special format. Example ABCD-1234 Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty Nothing grey appears inside the empty box. That is fine for plain questions."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-defaultValue","title":"Starts filled with","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Text already in the answer box when it opens. Leave blank when the player should type from scratch. What to put here Use this when editing an answer is faster than typing it from scratch. Example MSKU- Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty The answer box starts empty. That is the normal choice."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-password","title":"Mask typing","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Hides what the player types, like a password box. Use for passphrases, keys and private codes. What to put here Turn it on for secrets the player should not see echoed back on screen. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-storeAs","title":"Save answer as","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Optional name for the answer. If you type playerAnswer here, later nodes can read it with {{data.playerAnswer}}. Leave blank if only this choice matters. What to put here Use a short name such as verdict, handle or recoveryCode. Example recoveryCode Default empty If you leave it empty The answer is not kept after this node. That is fine when the outgoing wire is all you need. Watch out If later text says {{data.recoveryCode}}, this field must say recoveryCode."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-matchMode","title":"Accept","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Choose whether any submitted text continues, or whether the answer must match something you set. What to put here Pick Any submitted text for open questions. Pick one of the checked choices when a specific answer should unlock the next beat. Default \"any\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Watch out Once you choose a checked answer, the node grows a Wrong output. Wire it if a miss should do more than stop this beat. Choice What it is stored as Any submitted text any Exactly this answer exact Contains these words contains Matches a pattern regex"},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-expected","title":"Answer to accept","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"The text that counts as the right answer. Tags are filled before the check, so a saved password can be accepted too. Example ABCD-1234 Default empty When it appears Shown only when Accept is Exactly this answer , Contains these words or Matches a pattern . Until then the field is hidden. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty Every typed answer misses. Fill this in, or switch Accept back to Any submitted text. Watch out The accepted answer needs a clue somewhere in the quest. Without that, the player is guessing."},{"url":"nodes/fx-prompt.html#node-fx-prompt-field-caseSensitive","title":"Case sensitive","section":"nodes/fx-prompt","text":"Turn off to accept any capitalisation. Turn on only when upper and lower case are part of the puzzle. Default off Limits On or off. When it appears Shown only when Accept is Exactly this answer , Contains these words or Matches a pattern . Until then the field is hidden. Watch out Left on, apple and Apple are different answers. Keep it off unless capitals matter. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should happen right before the question appears. Submitted output The path after a submitted answer. When Accept checks for a specific answer, this is the Correct path. Wrong output The path for a submitted answer that does not match. This socket appears only after Accept chec"},{"url":"nodes/fx-retirequest.html","title":"Retire quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Retire quest Effects fx.retireQuest","section":"nodes/fx-retirequest.html","text":"Retire quest — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Retire quest Effects fx.retireQuest What it does Takes this quest out of the player's active list without marking it complete. When you'd use it Use it for fail states, cancelled jobs, or stories that should vanish without rewards. Do not use it for a normal success ending. Use Complete quest for that. It has no output because the current story path ends here. In the game, this looks like The quest disappears from the active list. It does not count as completed. Fields This node has nothing to configure. It marks a moment in the story and runs whate"},{"url":"nodes/fx-setdata.html#node-fx-setdata-field-key","title":"Key","section":"nodes/fx-setdata","text":"A name you choose, like doorCode. Use the same name later to read this value back. Example doorCode Default empty If you leave it empty The value is stored under no name, so nothing can find it again."},{"url":"nodes/fx-setdata.html#node-fx-setdata-field-value","title":"Value","section":"nodes/fx-setdata","text":"Any text is fine — a word, a number, an address. It is just stored, so nothing here can error. You can also insert a live value by typing {{data.name}} for something you saved earlier. Example 4417 Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . Watch out The name has to match exactly when you read it back. A typo reads as an empty value and nothing warns you. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same "},{"url":"nodes/fx-shell.html#node-fx-shell-field-command","title":"Command","section":"nodes/fx-shell","text":"The command executed in the player's terminal, as if they had typed it. Tags like {{data.targetIp}} are filled in first. What to put here The command to run. Tags work here, so you can drop in a value you stored earlier. Example nmap {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty There is nothing to run, so the node does nothing. Watch out It runs whether or not the player is ready for it. A command that needs an earlier step will fail in front of them. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this nod"},{"url":"nodes/fx-unclaimquest.html#node-fx-unclaimquest-field-questName","title":"Quest","section":"nodes/fx-unclaimquest","text":"The identifier of the claimed quest to remove. Leave blank to remove this quest. What to put here Copy the internal name from the quest strip or the other mod notes. Blank targets this quest. Example side-contract Default empty If you leave it empty The current quest is removed. Watch out A spelling mismatch removes nothing, so the unwanted quest stays active. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input The point where the chosen quest should be removed. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and"},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-amountMode","title":"Amount type","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"A fixed sum, or a slice of whatever the player currently has in the bank. What to put here Pick which of the two numbers below this node reads. Fixed amount reads Amount . Percentage of balance reads Percent . Default \"fixed\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Both number fields stay visible whichever you pick, and the one you did not pick is ignored. Check this first when a charge comes out wrong. Choice What it is stored as Fixed amount fixed Percentage of balance percent"},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-amount","title":"Amount","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"Dollars taken from the player's account. What to put here The sum to take, in dollars. Example 35 Default 100 Limits 0 or more. If you leave it empty Nothing happens. The charge is skipped with no warning and no line in the bank statement."},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-percent","title":"Percent","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"Percentage of the player's current balance, taken when this node runs. What to put here The share of the player's current balance to take. 10 takes a tenth of whatever they have right now. Default 10 Limits 0 to 100. Watch out The result is rounded to a whole number, so a small balance can produce a charge of zero."},{"url":"nodes/fx-withdraw.html#node-fx-withdraw-field-description","title":"Description","section":"nodes/fx-withdraw","text":"The label on the bank statement line. Example Tool licence — packet sniffer Default \"Purchase\" Limits Technically any text, but keep it short. A statement line is only a few words. If you leave it empty The statement line has no label, so the player sees money vanish from nowhere. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mism"},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-label","title":"Name","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"Shown in the frame's title bar — name the cluster after what it does, e.g. “Act 1: recon”. Default \"Group\""},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-color","title":"Title bar colour","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"Colour-code your frames however you like — e.g. one colour per act, or per character. It only changes how the frame looks in the editor. Default \"#64748b\""},{"url":"nodes/layout-group.html#node-layout-group-field-comment","title":"Comment","section":"nodes/layout-group","text":"A note to future-you about what this cluster does. What to put here Worth writing as though a stranger will read it, because they might. This text is the part of the frame that survives into the exported mod. Default empty Watch out A comment containing the two characters that close a block comment cannot break the export. The compiler escapes it for you. Wires This node has no sockets of its own. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The insp"},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-name","title":"Identifier","section":"nodes/objective","text":"A short unique name for this objective so other nodes can refer to it. Lowercase with dashes reads well. Example dock-shipment Default empty If you leave it empty Other nodes have nothing to refer to. Name the objective before you wire anything to it."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-description","title":"Shown to the player","section":"nodes/objective","text":"The line shown in the player's quest journal. A clear instruction tends to read well, but any wording that fits your quest works. What to put here Write it as an instruction: what to do, and where. Example Find the shipment manifest on the dock terminal. Default empty Limits Technically any text, but keep it to one line. The journal truncates a long one. If you leave it empty The journal shows a blank task. The player has no idea what they are meant to do."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-hint","title":"Hint","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Revealed only if the player asks for help. As gentle or as cryptic as you want it to be. Example The manifest sits on the machine by the gate, not the office."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-info","title":"Extra info","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Extra detail in the journal's expanded view. Good for lore or background. What to put here Lore belongs here rather than in the journal line. Example Meridian Capital moved the shipment twice after the strike."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-terminalCommand","title":"Suggested command","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Suggested command shown in the journal. It runs nothing — it is a copy-pasteable nudge. Example ssh dock01.meridian.local Watch out It runs nothing on its own. It is a nudge the player can copy, not a shortcut."},{"url":"nodes/objective.html#node-objective-field-hidden","title":"Hidden until unlocked","section":"nodes/objective","text":"Keep the objective out of the journal until another objective unlocks it. Use for twists. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the objective is handed to the player — usually Quest start or the objective before it. Trigger input The When event node that ticks this objective over. Unlocked by input An objective that has to finish before this one becomes available. On complete output Whatever should happen once the player finishes — the next objective, a payment, the ending. Unlocks output The objective this one opens up. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire o"},{"url":"nodes/pack-node.html","title":"Addon node — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Addon node Community addons pack.node","section":"nodes/pack-node.html","text":"Addon node — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Addon node Community addons pack.node What it does Runs a node supplied by a community addon instead of one built into the editor. When you'd use it Use it when an addon adds something the editor does not have. Addon nodes appear under Editor Mods in the palette. You need the addon installed. Without it the node has nothing to run, and the player misses whatever it was meant to do. In the game, this looks like Whatever the addon's node does, at the point the story reaches it. Fields This node has no fields of its own. Pick an installed addon and one o"},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-commandName","title":"Command name","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The terminal command the player runs, e.g. decrypt. It appears in help output. Example decrypt Default \"decrypt\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-commandDescription","title":"Help text","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The one-line description shown next to the command in help. Example Decrypt the intercepted payload Default \"Decrypt the intercepted payload\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-prompt","title":"Prompt","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The text printed before the cursor, e.g. \"Passphrase >\". Example Passphrase > Default \"Passphrase >\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-mask","title":"Mask the input","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Hides typing behind * — the player sees “Passphrase > ****” instead of their answer, like a password box. Use it when the answer is a secret. What to put here Turn this on to hide what the player types behind asterisks. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-matchMode","title":"Match","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Exactly equals checks the whole answer. Contains accepts it anywhere in what the player types. Matches pattern is for advanced authors who want to accept a whole family of answers at once. What to put here How the answer is checked. Default \"exact\" Limits One of the 3 listed below. Watch out A pattern match is the hardest of the three for a player to guess at. Use it only when several answers should count. Choice What it is stored as Exactly equals exact Contains contains Matches pattern regex"},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-expected","title":"Expected answer","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"The answer that counts as correct — or the pattern, if you chose “Matches pattern” above. Default empty If you leave it empty Nothing the player types can be correct, so the story never leaves the wrong path. Watch out The player has to be able to find this somewhere. An answer with no clue behind it is a guess, not a puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-caseSensitive","title":"Case sensitive","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Turn off to accept any capitalisation. Recommended unless case is part of the puzzle. Default off Limits On or off. Watch out Left on, a player who types the right word with the wrong capitals fails. Turn it off unless case is part of the puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-successMessage","title":"Success message","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Printed on a match, then the green “Correct” socket fires. Example Decrypted. Default \"Decrypted.\""},{"url":"nodes/reply-input.html#node-reply-input-field-failureMessage","title":"Failure message","section":"nodes/reply-input","text":"Printed on a miss, then the red “Wrong” socket fires. The player can run the command again. Default \"Wrong passphrase.\" Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before the player is asked. Correct output The path that runs when the answer matches. Wrong output The path that runs on a miss. The player can try again, so this is not usually the end. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add thi"},{"url":"nodes/trigger-event.html#node-trigger-event-field-event","title":"Game event","section":"nodes/trigger-event","text":"All supported HackHub events, listed with the details each one carries. Default empty If you leave it empty There is nothing to listen for, so the node never fires."},{"url":"nodes/trigger-event.html#node-trigger-event-field-conditions","title":"Only when","section":"nodes/trigger-event","text":"Leave empty to fire on any occurrence. What to put here Add one clause per thing that has to be true. Default an empty list Watch out The same action can raise more than one event. Check the list for the one that carries the details you need. Wires Socket Direction What to connect When output The objective or branch that should react when the event fires. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find When ev"},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-host","title":"Host IP","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The address the player points a database client at. Example {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-user","title":"Username","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The login sqlmap or a client uses. What to put here The login the player uses. Example admin Default \"admin\""},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-password","title":"Password","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The password. Give the player a way to find it — a config file, a leaked dump, a cracked hash. Default \"secret123\" Watch out The default is a placeholder. Leave it in place and the player can guess their way in without playing."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-tables","title":"Tables","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"The data inside: tables holding rows of named values, ready for the player's SQL. Default an empty list If you leave it empty The database exists and holds nothing, so there is nothing to find."},{"url":"nodes/world-database.html#node-world-database-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-database","text":"Drop the database when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Create database . Common mistakes No route to the password. The player finds the database and cannot open it. Hide the password somewhere they can reach. Messages you mi"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-domain","title":"Domain","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The hostname the player types, e.g. vault.corp-internal.net. Example vault.corp-internal.net Default empty If you leave it empty There is no name to resolve, so the node does nothing."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-ip","title":"Resolves to","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The address it resolves to. nslookup and dig will report this. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-vulnerabilities","title":"Vulnerabilities","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"Weaknesses on the host behind this name. In-game scanners report them when the player probes it. What to put here The weaknesses on the host behind this name. A scanner reports the ones you list. Default an empty list The button reads Add vulnerability ."},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-type","title":"Type","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The kind of weakness this machine has. The in-game scanners report it when the player probes the machine. What to put here The kind of weakness. Each one says what the player can do with it. Limits One of the 7 listed below. Choice What it is stored as SQL_INJECTION (steal from the database) SQL_INJECTION XSS (script in web pages) XSS CORS (read other sites' data) CORS SSRF (make the server fetch) SSRF LFI (read the server's files) LFI RFI (load a file from your URL) RFI RCE (run any command) RCE"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-version","title":"Version","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"The affected component's version, e.g. \"WordPress 5.8\". Cosmetic unless a trigger matches on it. Example WordPress 5.8"},{"url":"nodes/world-domain.html#node-world-domain-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-domain","text":"Drop the DNS entry when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Register domain . Common mistakes The name resolves to an address that does not exist. The player finds the hostname and reaches nothing. Point it at a machine your que"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-target","title":"Where","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Whose filesystem to write to. The player's own machine is written when the flow reaches this node; a remote device is mounted with the machine, under the account the player lands in, so the files are already there when they break in. Default \"player\" Limits One of the 2 listed below. Watch out Both fields below stay visible whichever you pick, and Device IP is only read for a remote device. Choice What it is stored as The player's PC player A remote device device"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-ip","title":"Device IP","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Only used for a remote device. Use the same {{data.targetIp}} token you gave the network, and the files are mounted on that machine before the player ever connects. What to put here The remote machine to write to. Only used when Where says a device. Example {{data.targetIp}} Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-parentPath","title":"Parent folder","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Where the files are mounted. Folders named etc, home, logs or lib are merged into the existing ones rather than replacing them. Example ~/docs Default \"~/\" Watch out Folders named etc, home, logs or lib are merged into the machine's real ones rather than created fresh."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-files","title":"Files","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The files and folders to create. A folder named etc, home, logs or lib merges with the machine's existing one. Default an empty list If you leave it empty Nothing is written. The button reads Add file ."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-name","title":"Name","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The file or folder name, exactly as it appears in ls. Example config"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-extension","title":"Extension","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The extension, e.g. txt, log, conf. Leave blank for folders. Example conf"},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-isFolder","title":"Folder","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Mark this entry as a directory rather than a file. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-hidden","title":"Hidden","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"Prefix the name with a dot so a plain ls does not show it. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-deleteable","title":"Player can delete","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The player can delete this file — rm works on it. Off by default, when placed files are protected. Use for evidence a quest tells the player to clean up. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-files.html#node-world-files-field-data","title":"Contents","section":"nodes/world-files","text":"The file's contents. This is where clues live — a config file, a log excerpt, a leaked password. Example DB_PASS=hunter2 Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. Step by step: add this node to a quest Find Place files under World building in the palette. Pick whose machine you are writing to. Se"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-ip","title":"Protected IP","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The machine these rules protect: the quest's own network, or one machine you name. Default \"{{data.targetIp}}\" Limits One of the 1 listed below. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . Choice What it is stored as Random — the quest's network {{data.targetIp}}"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Drop the firewall when the quest ends so the machine is reachable afterwards. Default on Limits On or off. Rule"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-port","title":"Port","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The port this rule applies to. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-allowed","title":"Allowed","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Off means the port is blocked by the firewall. What to put here Turn this on to let traffic through. Off blocks the port. Default off Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-source","title":"Source","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"Which machines the rule applies to: anywhere on the internet, or one machine you name. Default \"*\" Limits One of the 1 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Anywhere *"},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-destination","title":"Destination","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The machine the rule protects — usually the one in “Protected IP” above. Picking it copies that address; changing it later won't follow. Default \"{{data.targetIp}}\""},{"url":"nodes/world-firewall.html#node-world-firewall-field-locked","title":"Locked","section":"nodes/world-firewall","text":"The player cannot remove a locked rule. Limits On or off. Watch out A locked rule with no way around it stops the quest. Leave the player another route. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Firewall rule . Common mistakes The rule blocks the only way in. The player cannot f"},{"url":"nodes/world-network.html#node-world-network-field-device","title":"Devices","section":"nodes/world-network","text":"The router at the root of the network, plus everything behind it. Routers and splitters carry children; firewalls carry rules. What to put here The router at the root, and every machine behind it. The editor beside this field is where you add them. Default a starter set Watch out The player needs a route in. If the router exposes nothing and has no weakness, nothing behind it can be reached."},{"url":"nodes/world-network.html#node-world-network-field-destroyOnComplete","title":"Also remove this network when the quest is completed","section":"nodes/world-network","text":"Off by default: the network stays in the world after the quest, the way a real company would. It is also safer, because deleting a machine the player is still connected to can hang the game. Abandoning the quest removes it either way. What to put here Turn this on to take the network back out of the world when the quest completes. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. T"},{"url":"nodes/world-packdata.html","title":"Give data to an addon — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Give data to an addon Comm","section":"nodes/world-packdata.html","text":"Give data to an addon — HackHub Quest Editor handbook Give data to an addon Community addons world.packData What it does Hands a filled-in data shape to a community addon. When you'd use it Use it when an addon your quest depends on asks for data. Pick the pack, pick the shape, fill in what it wants. You need the addon installed to see its fields. Nothing appears here until it is. In the game, this looks like The addon receives the values you entered when the story reaches this node. Fields This node has no fields of its own. Pick an installed addon pack and one of its data shapes. The editor "},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-ip","title":"Device IP","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"A router IP or any device behind it. Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-action","title":"Action","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Open makes an existing port reachable. Close blocks it. Add creates a new service; Remove deletes it entirely. What to put here What to do to the port. Default \"open\" Limits One of the 4 listed below. Choice What it is stored as Open open Close close Add add Remove remove"},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.external","title":"External port","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"The port number as seen from outside — what nmap reports. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.internal","title":"Internal port","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"The port the service listens on inside the machine. Example 22 Default 22 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.service","title":"Service","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"What nmap prints next to the port, e.g. http, ssh, mysql. Example ssh Default \"ssh\""},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-port.active","title":"Open","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Turn off to make the port appear closed. Default on Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-port.html#node-world-port-field-restoreOnComplete","title":"Restore when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-port","text":"Put the port back the way it was when the quest ends. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Change port . Common mistakes The service name does not match the port number. A scan reporting ssh on port 80 tells the player nobody was paying attenti"},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-command","title":"Tool","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Pick the in-game command this overrides, like nmap or whois. When the player runs it on your input, they see your text instead of the usual result. Default \"nmap\" Limits One of the 11 listed below. Choice What it is stored as nmap nmap hydra hydra whois whois nslookup nslookup mxlookup mxlookup ping ping lynx lynx geoip geoip ssh ssh ftp ftp weechat weechat"},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-input","title":"Keyed by","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"What the player types after the command — the IP, domain or name it answers for. The tool only responds to this exact input. hydra/ssh/ftp use the user + target fields below instead. Example {{data.targetIp}} Default empty Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data . If you leave it empty There is nothing to match, so the response never fires."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-inputUser","title":"User","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Only match when the player ran the command against this user."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-inputTarget","title":"Target","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Only match when the player ran the command against this host. Tags work here Type {{data.something}} to insert a value you saved with Set quest data ."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-dataText","title":"Response","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"One “Label: value” per line. The editor turns them into exactly what that tool shows in-game — add a line for each thing you want the player to see. Example PORT: 22 STATE: open SERVICE: ssh Default empty If you leave it empty The command answers with nothing, which reads to the player as a dead host."},{"url":"nodes/world-toolresponse.html#node-world-toolresponse-field-removeOnComplete","title":"Remove when the quest ends","section":"nodes/world-toolresponse","text":"Stop intercepting the command when the quest ends. Default on Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should run immediately before this node. Out output Whatever should happen after this node runs. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection takes, mismatch them and it will not. The inspector for Tool response . Common mistakes The response answers anything. Key it to a specific input, or the player's first careless scan gives them the answer. The res"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-ssid","title":"Network name (SSID)","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The network name shown in the in-game Wi-Fi list. Example DOCKNET-5G Default \"NEIGHBOUR_5Ghz\" If you leave it empty The access point has no visible name, so the player cannot recognise the right target."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-password","title":"WPA passphrase","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The passphrase the player must discover. Make sure some node in your quest reveals it. Example forklift-1948 Default \"letmein123\" Watch out If the player must type or crack this value later, make sure they can earn it first."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-signal","title":"Signal strength","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The game's Wi-Fi scale: 0 = weakest, 3 = strongest. It also drives how long joining takes. What to put here How strong and quick to join the access point should feel. Default 2 Limits 0 to 3. Whole numbers, in steps of 1."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-bssid","title":"BSSID","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Optional MAC address shown for the access point. Leave blank to let the game pick one. Example 02:24:00:00:17:48 If you leave it empty The game picks one for you. Fill it only when a clue or test needs a fixed value. Watch out Current game builds may show no network name after Bettercap targets this value, but the attack still works."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-channel","title":"Channel","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Optional 2.4 GHz or 5 GHz channel shown by scans. Leave blank to let the game pick one for the SSID's band. Example 44 Limits 1 to 196. Whole numbers, in steps of 1. If you leave it empty The game chooses a channel that fits the network name."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-wps","title":"Advertises WPS","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Whether Wi-Fi scans show WPS for this access point. Leave off unless your story needs to pin it. What to put here Turn it on for targets that should look old, consumer-grade, or poorly locked down. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-model","title":"Router model","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Enables the in-game `fern` recovery route. Leave blank to disable it. Example TP-Link Archer C6 If you leave it empty The access point still exists. Leave this blank if router-model recovery is not part of the puzzle."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-users","title":"Router accounts","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Accounts on the access point's own system. Their files land in /home/<username>/. What to put here Accounts on the access point's own router. Use them for an admin-panel step, not for the machine behind it. Default an empty list The button reads Add account ."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-username","title":"Username","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The login name for ssh, ftp or a web login page. Example admin"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-password","title":"Password","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Leave blank to let the game pick one. Example forklift-1948 Default \"letmein123\" Watch out If the player must type or crack this value later, make sure they can earn it first."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-firstName","title":"First name","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Shown on the account's profile page and in whois results."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-lastName","title":"Last name","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Shown on the account's profile page and in whois results."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-emailAddress","title":"E-mail","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The account's e-mail address. Useful as a lead the player can mail. Example admin@docknet.internal"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-acceptReverseTCP","title":"Accepts reverse TCP","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Lets the player open a connection from this account back to their own machine. Turn on only if your quest needs it. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-ports","title":"Router ports","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Open ports on the access point itself. What to put here Services exposed by the access point's router itself. Default an empty list The button reads Add port ."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-external","title":"External port","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The port number as seen from outside. This is what nmap reports and what the player connects to. Example 80 Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-internal","title":"Internal port","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The port the service actually listens on inside the machine. Leave equal to the external port unless you are deliberately redirecting. Limits 0 to 65535."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-service","title":"Service","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"What nmap prints next to the port, e.g. http, ssh, ftp, mysql. Free text — it is a label, not a real service. Example http"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-version","title":"Version","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"The banner nmap -sV prints. Use three numbers and no letters — metasploit refuses \"7.2\" and \"7.2p2\", leaving the player unable to run the exploit. Blank omits the version line. Example nginx 8.4.71"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-active","title":"Open","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Closed ports show as filtered to nmap. Limits On or off."},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-children","title":"Devices behind the access point","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Machines reachable through this access point. Add a router here to build a second network hop. What to put here The machines the player reaches after joining the Wi-Fi. Default an empty list"},{"url":"nodes/world-wifi.html#node-world-wifi-field-destroyOnComplete","title":"Also remove this access point when the quest is completed","section":"nodes/world-wifi","text":"Off by default: the access point stays in the world after the quest. Abandoning the quest removes it either way. What to put here Turn this on for a temporary access point that should disappear when the job is done. Default off Limits On or off. Wires Socket Direction What to connect In input Whatever should happen before the access point exists. Out output The briefing, objective, or next setup step that assumes the access point is live. Then runs next When tests a condition Unlocks opens an objective Data carries a value A wire only joins two sockets of the same colour, and the editor draws the wire in that colour. That is what the colours are for: match the colours and the connection take"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-entry","title":"Quest lifecycle","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Quest start entry.start Runs once when the quest begins. Setup goes here. On start & reload entry.load Runs on claim and every load. Listeners go here. On quest complete entry.complete Runs after a Complete quest node, auto-complete, or Complete button finishes the quest. On quest abandoned entry.abandon Runs if the player gives up. Cleanup goes here."},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-objective","title":"Objectives","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Objective objective A task the player must complete"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-trigger","title":"Triggers","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does When event trigger.event React to something the player does"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-world","title":"World building","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Create network world.network Routers, devices, firewalls and ports Create Wi-Fi world.wifi A crackable access point Firewall rule world.firewall Allow or block a port Change port world.port Open, close, add or remove a port Register domain world.domain Point a hostname at an IP Create database world.database Content for sqlmap and DatabaseManager Place files world.files Drop files on a PC or a remote device Tool response world.toolResponse Script what a recon tool reports"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-comms","title":"Communication","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Dialogue comms.dialogue A conversation with the player — phone call, Kisscord, e-mail or WeeChat Twotter comms.tweet Post tweets from one of the mod's Twotter accounts — one, or a profile's worth of history"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-reply","title":"Custom terminal","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Manual input reply.input The player types a passphrase"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-effect","title":"Effects","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Pay the player fx.pay Deposit money Charge the player fx.withdraw Withdraw money Notify fx.notify A popup or toast Ask player fx.prompt Ask for one line of text Set quest data fx.setData Store a value for later Claim another quest fx.claimQuest Chain into the next quest Complete quest fx.completeQuest Finish the current quest and run On quest complete Retire quest fx.retireQuest Remove the current quest without marking it complete Unclaim quest fx.unclaimQuest Remove a claimed quest from the player's list Run terminal command fx.shell Execute in the terminal Open handbook fx.handbook Jump to an in-game article"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-community","title":"Community addons","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Give data to an addon world.packData Pick a data shape from an addon and fill it in Addon node pack.node A story node from an addon — find it under Editor Mods"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-flow","title":"Flow control","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does App Install Check flow.appcheck Route on whether the player has an app Branch flow.branch Split on a condition Wait flow.delay Pause before continuing Timer flow.timer Do something at a later in-game time Reroute flow.reroute Split or tidy a wire Random pick flow.random Choose one option at random Sequence flow.sequence Fire several outputs one after another Debug probe flow.debug Print what is happening here"},{"url":"nodes.html#cat-layout","title":"Layout","section":"nodes","text":"Node What it does Group frame layout.group A named box that moves its nodes Sticky note flow.note A comment on the canvas Story Beat flow.beat Plan a story beat — colour it, preview it ← How a quest works Feature guides → HackHub Quest Editor handbook · documents editor build 2026-10-03.r267 · every figure on these pages is taken from the editor's own source."},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#start-here","title":"What is happening?","section":"troubleshooting","text":"You see this Go to Nothing at all, in the editor or the game Nothing happens at all An objective stays open forever The objective never ticks The player cannot reach a machine you built The player cannot get in The export window says Needs attention The export report Words on a node you do not recognise Every message, quoted exactly Something else Frequently asked"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#nothing-runs","title":"Nothing happens at all","section":"troubleshooting","text":"Work down this list in order. Each step rules out the most common cause first. 1. Nothing is wired to a start A node only runs if a wire reaches it from Quest start or On start & reload . Select your first story node and look at its In socket. If nothing is attached, nothing will ever arrive. 2. The node is unreachable The canvas marks these. A node with an Unreachable badge is on the map but off the story. 3. It is a listener in the wrong place Anything that has to be listening belongs on On start & reload . Set it up on Quest start instead and it is gone the moment the player reloads. 4. You exported but did not unzip The game reads a folder in mods/ , not an archive. A .zip sitting in tha"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#objective-never-ticks","title":"The objective never ticks","section":"troubleshooting","text":"An objective completes one of two ways: the story flow reaches it, or the event wired to its trigger fires. If neither happens, it stays open forever, and the quest cannot be finished. Nothing is wired to its trigger The canvas calls this No trigger , and the quest's Health group counts them. This is the single most common reason a quest cannot be completed. The wire went into the wrong socket In takes a blue flow wire and ticks the objective when the story arrives. Trigger takes a cyan one and waits for the player. A wire into In cannot respond to anything the player does. You wired two events Only the first event wired to a trigger is used. The second is ignored, so if you added a backup, "},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#cannot-get-in","title":"The player cannot get into the machine","section":"troubleshooting","text":"You built a network and the player cannot reach it. Work through the doors in order. The router has no way in The device tree says so in plain words. This router has no way in: set a model for `fern`, or enable support-mail recovery. Neither route is open, so there is nothing to try. The exploit lands in the wrong account An SSH exploit lands in a guest account when it finds one. Your files may be in an account the player has not reached yet. Turn the exploit off for a machine that should hold only the accounts you listed. The address does not exist If a Place files node points at an address nothing uses, the message is red for a reason. The export drops those files rather than failing. Chec"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#export-refuses","title":"The export report","section":"troubleshooting","text":"First, the premise: the export does not refuse. The Download .zip button is disabled only while the file is being packed. It produces a .zip whatever the report says, because a quest with problems still deserves to be tested. What the report is telling you is what will change on the way into the game. Needs attention At least one item is an error. Something you built will be dropped or altered. Fix these before you ship. Good to know Everything exported as you built it, and what you built may not be what you meant. Read them, then decide. The report. It describes consequences; it does not block you. Each item is quoted in full, with its fix, in the report before you export . Paste the wordin"},{"url":"troubleshooting.html#faq","title":"Frequently asked","section":"troubleshooting","text":"I set a payment and the player got nothing An amount of 0 , or a blank field, does nothing at all — the node runs and pays nobody. Type a real number. A hundred dollars is a reasonable first fee. I paid a percentage and it came out as zero Percentages are rounded to whole dollars. Ten percent of four dollars is forty cents, which rounds to nothing. Use a fixed amount for small sums. My files are missing in the game Almost always an address mismatch. A Place files node aimed at a network your quest does not create has its files dropped by the export. The field turns red to say so. The quest panel shows {{data.targetIp}} instead of an address Tags are filled in when the quest starts. Seeing on"},{"url":"tutorial.html#tour","title":"The screen, labelled","section":"tutorial","text":"Open the editor with nothing loaded and you get a short welcome panel. Its own description of the job: Drag nodes from the left onto the canvas, then wire their sockets together. Every field on the right is a dropdown or a switch — no JSON, no code. That is the whole method, and the screen is arranged around it. The editor with a quest loaded. Five regions, and you use four of them constantly. Top bar The buttons: New , Load , Save , undo, redo, the quest tabs, Dialogues , Websites , Dry run , Addons , Settings and Export mod . Hover any of them for a longer description. Palette Every node the editor can make, grouped by what it does. Type in the Filter nodes box to narrow it. Drag one onto "},{"url":"tutorial.html#new-project","title":"Start from a template","section":"tutorial","text":"Fourteen templates come with the editor, including a blank quest and a 36-node quest. You want First Contact : eight nodes, marked Beginner, and deliberately no hacking in it. Its own description: The whole spine in miniature: a brief, one objective (a person lookup), a payment and a closing line. No hacking — just the shape of a quest. Click Browse 14 templates on the welcome panel. If you closed it, click Templates in the top bar instead. In the window titled Start from a template , choose First Contact . The editor confirms with a message reading Loaded “First Contact”. The template picker. Difficulty and node count are shown for each. Save before replacing the project. New asks Start a n"},{"url":"tutorial.html#read-the-map","title":"Read the map you loaded","section":"tutorial","text":"Before changing anything, work out what this quest does. Zoom out until all eight nodes fit on screen. Read left to right. The wires, not the positions, decide the order. Follow the blue wires. They read as \"and then\", so a chain of them is the story running in order. You should find a brief being sent to the player, an objective, a payment, and a closing line. That is the spine, and almost every quest you write will be this shape with more in the middle. Now click the objective node. The inspector opens on its fields. The three inspector tabs. Node is the one you will use most. Why this matters. A template you cannot read is a template you cannot edit. Every quest you build later will be a "},{"url":"tutorial.html#add-objective","title":"Add your own objective","section":"tutorial","text":"Now change the quest. You will add a second objective, so the player has two things to do instead of one. In the palette, find Objective . Type objective in Filter nodes if the list is long. Drag it onto an empty part of the canvas. A new node appears. Click it. The inspector now shows its fields. Objective, in the palette. Fill in the two fields that matter for this exercise: Identifier A short name the game uses internally, like find-the-contact . The player never sees it, but it has to be unique within the quest. Shown to the player The line that appears in their quest panel. Write it as an instruction: Look up who wrote the article. Two fields, and the objective exists. Now wire it in. D"},{"url":"tutorial.html#wire-trigger","title":"Make it complete","section":"tutorial","text":"An objective with nothing wired to its trigger never completes on its own. This is the step that turns a quest from a script into something that reacts to the player. Drag When event from the palette onto the canvas. Click it and open its Game event field. Pick the event that matches what the player will do. Drag from the node's When socket to the objective's Trigger socket. The wire is cyan, because it is a different kind of connection from the blue ones. Mid-drag. The wire follows your cursor until you drop it on a matching socket. Choosing the event. There are 99, grouped by the part of the game they come from. The socket has to match. A cyan wire only lands on a cyan socket. If you drop "},{"url":"tutorial.html#write-the-reward","title":"Give the player a reason to finish","section":"tutorial","text":"Two nodes make a quest feel like a job rather than a checklist. Both are already in First Contact; click them to see what they hold. A Dialogue node is a conversation on the player's phone. Select one and the inspector shows who speaks and what they say. This is where an employer explains the job and where the story closes. A conversation, node by node. A Pay the player node puts money in their account. Set the amount in dollars. It starts at 100. Adjust the amount to suit the job. The payment. Leave the amount blank and the node does nothing."},{"url":"tutorial.html#check-it","title":"Check it before you ship it","section":"tutorial","text":"The editor checks your work in two places. The status bar along the bottom tells you the shape of what you have built. The status bar. It reports counts and whether your work is saved; it does not list problems. Problems show up on the nodes themselves and in the export window, not here. The fuller check is Dry run , which walks through your quest the way the exported mod would run it. Dry run. It is the closest thing to playing the quest without installing it. Run it and watch the order. If a node you expected never appears, it is not wired to anything. Drop a Debug probe after it and run again. The probe prints what a node does when it fires. It also shows whether the node fires at all. Ch"},{"url":"tutorial.html#export","title":"Export, install, play","section":"tutorial","text":"Click Export mod . Before packing, the window shows what you are about to ship. It lists your quests, websites, the permissions your nodes need, and every file in the archive. The export summary. Read it before you download. Anything red means the game will drop or change something you built. Fix it first. Then choose Download .zip and unzip the folder into the game's mods/ directory. Everything the archive contains and why is on the exporting and installing page. The one thing worth remembering from it: the .zip is finished output. Your project file is the thing you keep editing."},{"url":"tutorial.html#recap","title":"What you now know","section":"tutorial","text":"You loaded a quest and read what it did. You added to it, made an objective respond to real play, and installed the result. Four ideas carried all of it: Wires decide order, not position. Moving a node changes nothing. Your quest map explains why. Wires have kinds. Blue for \"and then\", cyan for \"when\". A socket takes only its own kind. An objective completes when its trigger fires. That is the difference between a quest that reacts and one that assumes. Exporting is not saving. Two different files, two different purposes. Next, take apart a bigger template. The Byline adds a website clue, and Cold Call tells its story entirely in conversation. Then look at the How do I… pages when you want t"}];
